WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:05.736 --> 00:08.866 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference July 27,

00:09.116 --> 00:15.416 align:middle
2022 CHAIR POWELL.

00:15.846 --> 00:17.356 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:17.776 --> 00:19.986 align:middle
My colleagues and I
are strongly committed

00:19.986 --> 00:21.566 align:middle
to bringing inflation back down,

00:21.866 --> 00:24.096 align:middle
and we're moving
expeditiously to do so.

00:25.346 --> 00:28.176 align:middle
We have both the tools we need
and the resolve it will take

00:28.176 --> 00:29.836 align:middle
to restore price
stability on behalf

00:29.836 --> 00:31.596 align:middle
of American families
and businesses.

00:32.136 --> 00:34.906 align:middle
The economy and the country
have been through a lot

00:34.966 --> 00:37.546 align:middle
over the past two and a half
years and have proved resilient.

00:38.966 --> 00:40.616 align:middle
It is essential that
we bring inflation

00:40.616 --> 00:44.296 align:middle
down to our 2 percent goal if we
are to have a sustained period

00:44.296 --> 00:46.826 align:middle
of strong labor market
conditions that benefit all.

00:47.816 --> 00:51.156 align:middle
From the standpoint of
our congressional mandate

00:51.156 --> 00:53.296 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and price stability,

00:54.076 --> 00:55.796 align:middle
the current picture
is plain to see:

00:57.176 --> 00:58.826 align:middle
The labor market
is extremely tight,

00:59.276 --> 01:00.846 align:middle
and inflation is much too high.

01:01.336 --> 01:06.166 align:middle
Against this backdrop, today the
FOMC raised its policy interest

01:06.166 --> 01:09.716 align:middle
rate by   of a percentage
point and anticipates

01:09.716 --> 01:11.836 align:middle
that ongoing increases
in the target range

01:11.836 --> 01:13.936 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate will be appropriate.

01:15.986 --> 01:18.226 align:middle
In addition, we are
continuing the process

01:18.226 --> 01:20.756 align:middle
of significantly reducing the
size of our balance sheet.

01:21.066 --> 01:24.506 align:middle
And I'll have more to say about
today's monetary policy actions

01:25.236 --> 01:27.376 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

01:30.316 --> 01:32.726 align:middle
Recent indicators of spending
and production have softened.

01:33.366 --> 01:35.676 align:middle
Growth in consumer spending
has slowed significantly,

01:36.546 --> 01:39.356 align:middle
in part reflecting lower
real disposable income

01:39.356 --> 01:40.856 align:middle
and tighter financial
conditions.

01:42.476 --> 01:44.266 align:middle
Activity in the housing
sector has weakened,

01:44.676 --> 01:46.436 align:middle
in part reflecting
higher mortgage rates.

01:47.756 --> 01:50.346 align:middle
And after a strong increase
in the first quarter,

01:50.346 --> 01:53.096 align:middle
business fixed investment
also looks to have declined

01:53.406 --> 01:54.206 align:middle
in the second quarter.

01:56.556 --> 01:57.606 align:middle
Despite these developments,

01:57.606 --> 01:59.736 align:middle
the labor market has
remained extremely tight,

01:59.906 --> 02:02.066 align:middle
with the unemployment
rate near a 50-year low,

02:02.606 --> 02:06.546 align:middle
job vacancies near historical
highs, and wage growth elevated.

02:07.976 --> 02:10.706 align:middle
Over the past three months,
employment rose by an average

02:10.706 --> 02:14.406 align:middle
of 375,000 jobs per month-down

02:14.406 --> 02:16.036 align:middle
from the average
pace seen earlier

02:16.036 --> 02:17.656 align:middle
in the year, but still robust.

02:19.996 --> 02:22.366 align:middle
Improvements in labor market
conditions have been widespread,

02:22.576 --> 02:24.196 align:middle
including for workers
at the lower end

02:24.196 --> 02:25.976 align:middle
of the wage distribution as well

02:25.976 --> 02:28.086 align:middle
as for African Americans
and Hispanics.

02:30.046 --> 02:31.366 align:middle
Labor demand is very strong,

02:32.016 --> 02:33.716 align:middle
while labor supply
remains subdued,

02:34.156 --> 02:36.096 align:middle
with the labor force
participation rate little

02:36.096 --> 02:37.516 align:middle
changed since January.

02:39.756 --> 02:42.296 align:middle
Overall, the continued strength
of the labor market suggests

02:42.296 --> 02:44.916 align:middle
that underlying aggregate
demand remains solid.

02:46.966 --> 02:49.976 align:middle
Inflation remains well above our
longer-run goal of 2 percent.

02:50.916 --> 02:52.686 align:middle
Over the 12 months
ending in May,

02:53.426 --> 02:55.966 align:middle
total PCE prices
rose 6.3 percent;

02:56.436 --> 02:58.826 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

03:00.296 --> 03:02.966 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
4.7 percent.

03:03.796 --> 03:07.866 align:middle
In June, the 12-month change in
the consumer price index came

03:07.866 --> 03:11.426 align:middle
in above expectations at
9.1 percent, and the change

03:11.426 --> 03:13.976 align:middle
in the core CPI was 5.9 percent.

03:15.126 --> 03:16.636 align:middle
Notwithstanding the
recent slowdown

03:16.636 --> 03:19.556 align:middle
in overall economic activity,
aggregate demand appears

03:19.556 --> 03:22.466 align:middle
to remain strong, supply
constraints have been larger

03:22.466 --> 03:24.086 align:middle
and longer lasting
than anticipated,

03:24.576 --> 03:27.116 align:middle
and price pressures are
evident across a broad range

03:27.116 --> 03:28.146 align:middle
of goods and services.

03:30.486 --> 03:32.606 align:middle
Although prices for some
commodities have turned

03:32.606 --> 03:35.836 align:middle
down recently, the earlier
surge in prices of crude oil

03:35.836 --> 03:38.446 align:middle
and other commodities that
resulted from Russia's war

03:38.446 --> 03:42.046 align:middle
on Ukraine has boosted
prices for gasoline and food,

03:42.286 --> 03:44.646 align:middle
creating additional upward
pressure on inflation.

03:46.696 --> 03:48.686 align:middle
The Fed's monetary
policy actions are guided

03:48.686 --> 03:51.056 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

03:51.056 --> 03:52.986 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

03:54.496 --> 03:56.296 align:middle
My colleagues and
I are acutely aware

03:56.296 --> 03:58.996 align:middle
that high inflation imposes
significant hardship,

03:59.426 --> 04:02.156 align:middle
especially on those least
able to meet the higher costs

04:02.156 --> 04:04.966 align:middle
of essentials like food,
housing, and transportation.

04:06.416 --> 04:08.916 align:middle
We are highly attentive to
the risks high inflation poses

04:08.916 --> 04:12.116 align:middle
to both sides of our mandate,
and we are strongly committed

04:12.116 --> 04:14.466 align:middle
to returning inflation to
our 2 percent objective.

04:16.626 --> 04:17.466 align:middle
At today's meeting,

04:17.616 --> 04:20.406 align:middle
the Committee raised the target
range for the federal funds rate

04:20.706 --> 04:23.596 align:middle
by   of a percentage point,
bringing the target range

04:23.646 --> 04:25.616 align:middle
to 2¼ to 2½ percent.

04:26.556 --> 04:27.976 align:middle
And we're continuing the process

04:27.976 --> 04:30.346 align:middle
of significantly reducing the
size of our balance sheet,

04:30.846 --> 04:32.236 align:middle
which plays an important role

04:32.586 --> 04:34.416 align:middle
in firming the stance
of monetary policy.

04:36.316 --> 04:39.226 align:middle
Over coming months, we will be
looking for compelling evidence

04:39.226 --> 04:40.566 align:middle
that inflation is moving down,

04:40.646 --> 04:42.886 align:middle
consistent with inflation
returning to 2 percent.

04:44.476 --> 04:47.126 align:middle
We anticipate that ongoing
increases in the target range

04:47.126 --> 04:48.876 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate will be appropriate;

04:50.166 --> 04:52.596 align:middle
the pace of those increases
will continue to depend

04:53.266 --> 04:55.956 align:middle
on the incoming data
and the evolving outlook

04:55.956 --> 04:56.676 align:middle
for the economy.

04:58.036 --> 05:01.416 align:middle
Today's increase is the-in the
target range is the second 75

05:01.416 --> 05:03.486 align:middle
basis point increase
in as many meetings.

05:04.526 --> 05:07.996 align:middle
While another unusually large
increase could be appropriate

05:08.316 --> 05:11.516 align:middle
at our next meeting, that is
a decision that will depend

05:11.516 --> 05:13.576 align:middle
on the data we get
between now and then.

05:13.846 --> 05:17.806 align:middle
We will continue to make our
decisions meeting by meeting

05:18.196 --> 05:21.096 align:middle
and communicating-and
communicate our thinking

05:21.096 --> 05:22.336 align:middle
as clearly as possible.

05:22.896 --> 05:26.536 align:middle
As the stance of monetary
policy tightens further,

05:27.046 --> 05:29.646 align:middle
it likely will become
appropriate to slow the pace

05:29.646 --> 05:33.106 align:middle
of increases while we assess
how our cumulative policy

05:33.106 --> 05:35.396 align:middle
adjustments are affecting
the economy and inflation.

05:36.476 --> 05:40.266 align:middle
Our overarching focus is using
our tools to bring demand

05:40.266 --> 05:43.606 align:middle
into better balance with supply
in order to bring inflation back

05:43.646 --> 05:44.936 align:middle
down to our 2 percent goal

05:45.476 --> 05:47.716 align:middle
and to keep longer-term
inflation expectations

05:47.786 --> 05:48.396 align:middle
well anchored.

05:50.806 --> 05:52.386 align:middle
Making appropriate
monetary policy

05:52.386 --> 05:54.866 align:middle
in this uncertain environment
requires a recognition

05:55.066 --> 05:57.576 align:middle
that the economy
often involves-evolves

05:57.676 --> 05:58.766 align:middle
in unexpected ways.

06:00.066 --> 06:02.386 align:middle
Inflation has obviously
surprised to the upside

06:02.386 --> 06:05.166 align:middle
over the past year, and further
surprises could be in store.

06:06.356 --> 06:08.416 align:middle
We therefore will need to
be nimble in responding

06:08.416 --> 06:10.436 align:middle
to incoming data and
the evolving outlook.

06:10.896 --> 06:14.156 align:middle
And we will strive to
avoid adding uncertainty

06:14.156 --> 06:16.496 align:middle
in what is already an
extraordinarily challenging

06:16.656 --> 06:17.586 align:middle
and uncertain time.

06:19.206 --> 06:22.326 align:middle
We are highly attentive to
inflation risks and determined

06:22.326 --> 06:24.616 align:middle
to take the measures
necessary to return inflation

06:24.616 --> 06:26.296 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal.

06:27.526 --> 06:29.936 align:middle
This process is likely
to involve a period

06:29.936 --> 06:33.206 align:middle
of below-trend economic
growth and some softening

06:33.206 --> 06:34.386 align:middle
in labor market conditions.

06:34.986 --> 06:37.006 align:middle
But such outcomes
are likely necessary

06:37.006 --> 06:39.976 align:middle
to restore price stability
and to set the stage

06:39.976 --> 06:41.626 align:middle
for achieving maximum employment

06:41.626 --> 06:43.476 align:middle
and stable prices
over the longer run.

06:44.846 --> 06:48.676 align:middle
To conclude: We understand that
our actions affect communities,

06:48.676 --> 06:50.476 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

06:51.556 --> 06:53.936 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

06:55.236 --> 06:57.056 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

06:57.686 --> 06:59.146 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

06:59.146 --> 07:00.386 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

07:01.336 --> 07:01.696 align:middle
Thank you.

07:01.786 --> 07:03.046 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

07:03.776 --> 07:04.136 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:04.136 --> 07:05.366 align:middle
Let's start with Rachel.

07:06.646 --> 07:08.036 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

07:09.036 --> 07:09.786 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

07:09.786 --> 07:10.976 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

07:11.176 --> 07:12.856 align:middle
Rachel Siegel from
the Washington Post.

07:12.976 --> 07:15.396 align:middle
I'm wondering if you can
walk us through your thinking

07:15.536 --> 07:17.186 align:middle
around the decision not to go

07:17.186 --> 07:19.816 align:middle
for a full percentage
point increase.

07:20.506 --> 07:24.086 align:middle
We saw a ramp-up after the
May CPI report came in hotter

07:24.086 --> 07:26.876 align:middle
than usual and then, obviously,
the June figure did, too.

07:26.876 --> 07:29.476 align:middle
Was there any discussion of a
stronger hike at this meeting?

07:30.496 --> 07:31.056 align:middle
Yes. Thank you.

07:31.316 --> 07:31.696 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:31.696 --> 07:35.586 align:middle
Sure. So we did judge that a
75 basis point increase was the

07:35.586 --> 07:38.746 align:middle
right magnitude in light of
the data and in the context

07:38.746 --> 07:41.216 align:middle
of the ongoing increases
in the policy rate

07:41.216 --> 07:41.896 align:middle
that we've been making.

07:42.336 --> 07:44.086 align:middle
I'd say that we wouldn't
hesitate to,

07:44.146 --> 07:46.496 align:middle
to make an even larger
move than we did today

07:46.496 --> 07:48.506 align:middle
if the Committee were to
conclude that that was-that

07:48.506 --> 07:49.356 align:middle
that were appropriate.

07:50.066 --> 07:51.436 align:middle
That was not the
case at this meeting.

07:51.436 --> 07:54.326 align:middle
There was very broad support
for the move that we made.

07:55.256 --> 07:56.686 align:middle
You mentioned the-the
June meeting.

07:56.686 --> 07:59.816 align:middle
We, we had said many times
that we were prepared

07:59.816 --> 08:01.536 align:middle
to move aggressively,
more aggressively,

08:01.536 --> 08:03.196 align:middle
if inflation continued
to disappoint.

08:03.796 --> 08:05.956 align:middle
And that's why we did move
to a more aggressive pace

08:05.956 --> 08:07.616 align:middle
at the June meeting,
as we said we would do.

08:08.166 --> 08:10.796 align:middle
At this meeting, we continued
at that more aggressive pace,

08:10.876 --> 08:12.746 align:middle
as inflation has
continued to disappoint,

08:12.856 --> 08:14.436 align:middle
in the form of the
June CPI reading.

08:15.386 --> 08:15.686 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

08:16.026 --> 08:20.156 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

08:20.776 --> 08:23.406 align:middle
Thank you so much for
taking our questions.

08:23.406 --> 08:25.986 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the Financial Times.

08:26.476 --> 08:29.116 align:middle
As the Committee considers
the policy path forward,

08:29.286 --> 08:32.336 align:middle
how will it weigh the expected
decline in headline inflation,

08:32.866 --> 08:35.746 align:middle
which might come as a result of
the drop in commodity prices,

08:36.046 --> 08:39.796 align:middle
against the fact that we are
likely to see some persistence

08:40.366 --> 08:42.856 align:middle
in core readings, in particular?

08:43.376 --> 08:46.556 align:middle
And given that potential
tension and signs of, you know,

08:46.606 --> 08:49.986 align:middle
any kind of activity
weakening here,

08:50.326 --> 08:53.816 align:middle
how has the Committee's
thinking changed on how far

08:53.816 --> 08:58.976 align:middle
into restrictive territory
rates might need to go?

08:59.086 --> 08:59.676 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:59.676 --> 09:04.366 align:middle
So I guess I'd start by saying
we've been saying we would move

09:04.366 --> 09:06.636 align:middle
expeditiously to get to
the range of neutral.

09:06.636 --> 09:07.716 align:middle
And I think we've done that now.

09:07.716 --> 09:10.176 align:middle
We're at-we're at 2.25 to 2.5
[percent], and that's right

09:10.176 --> 09:11.676 align:middle
in the range of what
we think is neutral.

09:11.676 --> 09:15.606 align:middle
So the question is, how are we
thinking about the path forward?

09:15.856 --> 09:19.056 align:middle
So one thing that hasn't
changed is that-won't change-is

09:19.056 --> 09:22.186 align:middle
that our focus is continuing
to-is going to continue to be

09:22.186 --> 09:25.936 align:middle
on using our tools to bring
demand back into better balance

09:25.936 --> 09:27.986 align:middle
with supply in order to
bring inflation back down.

09:28.016 --> 09:30.366 align:middle
That'll continue to be
our overarching focus.

09:30.866 --> 09:33.776 align:middle
We also said that we expect
ongoing rate hikes will be

09:33.776 --> 09:36.206 align:middle
appropriate and that we'll make
decisions meeting by meeting.

09:36.756 --> 09:38.596 align:middle
So what are we going
to be looking at?

09:39.356 --> 09:40.876 align:middle
You know, we'll be looking
at the incoming data,

09:40.876 --> 09:43.586 align:middle
as I mentioned, and that'll
start with economic activity.

09:43.586 --> 09:46.736 align:middle
Are we seeing the slowdown
that we-the slowdown

09:46.736 --> 09:50.216 align:middle
in economic activity-that
we think we need?

09:50.466 --> 09:53.036 align:middle
And there's some evidence
that we are at this time.

09:53.676 --> 09:55.986 align:middle
Of course, we'll be looking
at labor market conditions.

09:55.986 --> 10:00.616 align:middle
And we'll be asking whether we
see the alignment between supply

10:00.616 --> 10:02.606 align:middle
and demand getting
better, getting closer.

10:03.006 --> 10:04.906 align:middle
Of course, we'll be looking
closely at inflation.

10:04.906 --> 10:06.256 align:middle
You mentioned headline and core.

10:06.696 --> 10:09.236 align:middle
Our mandate is for headline,
of course; it's not for core.

10:09.236 --> 10:11.086 align:middle
But we look at core
because core is,

10:11.176 --> 10:13.346 align:middle
is actually a better
indicator of headline

10:13.606 --> 10:15.036 align:middle
and of all inflation
going forward.

10:15.036 --> 10:17.106 align:middle
So we'll be-we'll
be looking at both.

10:17.336 --> 10:19.936 align:middle
And we'll be looking at
them for-at those both,

10:20.016 --> 10:22.886 align:middle
really-for what they're saying
about the outlook rather

10:22.886 --> 10:25.316 align:middle
than just simply for,
for what they say.

10:25.586 --> 10:27.606 align:middle
But we'll be asking:
Do we see inflationary

10:27.606 --> 10:28.616 align:middle
pressures declining?

10:28.616 --> 10:31.316 align:middle
Do we see actual readings
of inflation coming down?

10:31.416 --> 10:32.526 align:middle
So, in light of all that data,

10:32.906 --> 10:34.856 align:middle
the question we'll be
asking is whether the stance

10:34.856 --> 10:37.216 align:middle
of policy we have is
sufficiently restrictive

10:37.526 --> 10:39.696 align:middle
to bring inflation back down
to our 2 percent target.

10:40.476 --> 10:41.936 align:middle
And it's also worth noting

10:41.936 --> 10:43.566 align:middle
that these rate hikes
have been large,

10:43.566 --> 10:45.686 align:middle
and they've come-they've
come quickly.

10:45.796 --> 10:49.956 align:middle
And it's likely that their
full effect has not been felt

10:49.956 --> 10:50.556 align:middle
by the economy.

10:50.556 --> 10:52.716 align:middle
So there's probably some
additional tightening,

10:52.766 --> 10:55.196 align:middle
significant additional
tightening, in the pipeline.

10:55.916 --> 10:57.556 align:middle
So where are we going with this?

10:57.556 --> 11:01.026 align:middle
I think the best-I think
the Committee broadly feels

11:01.496 --> 11:04.456 align:middle
that we need to get
policy to at least

11:04.456 --> 11:05.986 align:middle
to a moderately restrictive
level.

11:06.586 --> 11:09.406 align:middle
And maybe the best data point
for that would be what we wrote

11:09.406 --> 11:11.626 align:middle
down in our SEP at
the-at the June meeting.

11:12.066 --> 11:14.326 align:middle
So I think the median [federal
funds rate] for the end

11:14.326 --> 11:15.876 align:middle
of this year, the
median would've been

11:15.876 --> 11:18.146 align:middle
between 3¼ and 3½ [percent].

11:18.146 --> 11:22.216 align:middle
And, then, people wrote
down 50 basis points higher

11:22.216 --> 11:23.906 align:middle
than that for 2023.

11:23.906 --> 11:27.896 align:middle
So that's-even though that's
now six weeks old, I guess,

11:27.896 --> 11:29.466 align:middle
that's-that's the
most recent reading.

11:29.466 --> 11:30.906 align:middle
Of course, we'll
update that reading

11:30.906 --> 11:33.146 align:middle
at the-at the September
meeting in eight weeks.

11:33.956 --> 11:35.046 align:middle
So that's how we're
thinking about it.

11:35.046 --> 11:37.366 align:middle
As I mentioned, as it
relates to September,

11:38.046 --> 11:40.986 align:middle
I said that another unusually
large increase could be

11:40.986 --> 11:43.076 align:middle
appropriate, but that's not
a decision we're making now.

11:43.076 --> 11:44.956 align:middle
It's one that we'll make
based on the data we see.

11:45.566 --> 11:48.256 align:middle
And we're going to be making
decisions meeting by meeting.

11:48.256 --> 11:51.636 align:middle
We think it's-we think
it's time to just go

11:51.636 --> 11:55.246 align:middle
to a meeting-by-meeting basis
and not provide, you know,

11:55.246 --> 11:57.666 align:middle
the kind of clear guidance
that we had provided

11:57.666 --> 11:58.536 align:middle
on the way to neutral.

11:59.786 --> 12:02.586 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

12:02.826 --> 12:03.836 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

12:04.286 --> 12:05.666 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

12:06.326 --> 12:08.696 align:middle
Chair Powell, you've said
that your policy works

12:08.696 --> 12:12.826 align:middle
through influencing expectations
and that policy needs to be

12:12.856 --> 12:14.466 align:middle
at least moderately restrictive,

12:14.466 --> 12:16.946 align:middle
which means you need financial
conditions to stay tight.

12:17.716 --> 12:21.136 align:middle
Futures market pricing currently
implies you will raise rates

12:21.136 --> 12:23.796 align:middle
this year along the
lines of your June SEP

12:24.346 --> 12:27.626 align:middle
but then lower them a few
months later next year.

12:28.186 --> 12:31.016 align:middle
Are these expectations
consistent with the need

12:31.526 --> 12:34.536 align:middle
to keep financial
conditions tight in order

12:34.536 --> 12:36.066 align:middle
to moderate purchasing power

12:36.066 --> 12:37.846 align:middle
and bring inflation
back to 2 percent?

12:37.936 --> 12:38.896 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:39.136 --> 12:43.186 align:middle
So I'm going to start by
pointing out that it's very hard

12:43.186 --> 12:44.426 align:middle
to say with any confidence

12:44.426 --> 12:47.806 align:middle
in normal times-in normal
times-what the economy's going

12:47.906 --> 12:49.466 align:middle
to be doing in 6 or 12 months.

12:50.156 --> 12:53.586 align:middle
And to try to predict what the
appropriate monetary policy

12:53.586 --> 12:55.226 align:middle
would respond-response
would be-of course,

12:55.226 --> 12:57.166 align:middle
we do that in the
SEP, but, nonetheless,

12:57.166 --> 13:00.796 align:middle
you've got to take any estimates
of what rates will be next year

13:01.406 --> 13:03.546 align:middle
with a grain of salt, because
there's so much uncertainty.

13:03.546 --> 13:04.596 align:middle
These are not normal times.

13:04.596 --> 13:06.936 align:middle
There's significantly
more uncertainty now

13:06.936 --> 13:09.656 align:middle
about the path ahead than I
think there ordinarily is, and,

13:09.656 --> 13:10.756 align:middle
ordinarily, it's quite high.

13:11.346 --> 13:15.716 align:middle
So, again, I would-the best
data, the only data point I have

13:15.716 --> 13:17.526 align:middle
for you really is the June SEP,

13:18.396 --> 13:21.056 align:middle
which I think is just
the most recent thing

13:21.056 --> 13:22.046 align:middle
that the Committee's done.

13:22.606 --> 13:25.086 align:middle
Since then, inflation
has come in higher;

13:25.186 --> 13:28.896 align:middle
economic activity has come
in weaker than expected.

13:29.466 --> 13:32.616 align:middle
But at the same time, I would
say that's probably the best

13:32.616 --> 13:35.186 align:middle
estimate of where the
Committee's thinking is still,

13:35.546 --> 13:39.846 align:middle
which is that we would get to
a moderately restrictive level

13:39.846 --> 13:42.426 align:middle
by the end of this year-by
which I mean somewhere between 3

13:42.426 --> 13:45.636 align:middle
and 3½ percent-and that when
the Committee sees further rate

13:45.636 --> 13:51.046 align:middle
increases in 2023, as I
mentioned, we'll update that,

13:51.046 --> 13:52.306 align:middle
of course, at the
September meeting.

13:52.746 --> 13:55.586 align:middle
But that's really the
best I can do on that.

13:55.586 --> 13:56.296 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

13:56.696 --> 13:58.776 align:middle
You said inflation had
been a little bit hotter

13:59.106 --> 14:00.116 align:middle
than anticipated.

14:00.396 --> 14:03.566 align:middle
Has your view of the terminal
rate changed since June?

14:04.396 --> 14:05.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:05.506 --> 14:08.656 align:middle
So I wouldn't say it was-I think
we didn't expect a good reading,

14:08.696 --> 14:10.976 align:middle
but this one-this
one was even worse

14:10.976 --> 14:11.986 align:middle
than expected, I would say.

14:12.166 --> 14:15.536 align:middle
I don't talk about my
own personal estimate of,

14:15.536 --> 14:17.206 align:middle
of what the terminal
rate would be.

14:17.746 --> 14:22.336 align:middle
I do-I will write down that
in-it's going to evolve.

14:22.336 --> 14:24.656 align:middle
Obviously, it has evolved
over the course-I think,

14:24.656 --> 14:27.536 align:middle
for all participants, it
has evolved over the course

14:27.536 --> 14:30.436 align:middle
of the year as we learn how
persistent inflation's going

14:30.436 --> 14:30.756 align:middle
to be.

14:31.156 --> 14:32.646 align:middle
And by the time of
the September meeting,

14:32.646 --> 14:34.916 align:middle
we will have seen
two more CPI readings

14:34.916 --> 14:36.456 align:middle
and two more labor
market readings

14:36.976 --> 14:41.236 align:middle
and a significant amount of
readings about economic activity

14:41.236 --> 14:43.796 align:middle
and perhaps geopolitical
developments, who knows.

14:43.796 --> 14:45.816 align:middle
It'll be-it'll be a lot.

14:45.816 --> 14:47.546 align:middle
It's an eight-week
intermeeting period,

14:47.546 --> 14:49.316 align:middle
so I think we'll see
quite a lot of data.

14:49.466 --> 14:52.976 align:middle
And we'll make our decision at
that meeting based on that data.

14:52.976 --> 14:53.386 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:53.506 --> 14:55.076 align:middle
Jeanna. JEANNA SMIALEK.

14:55.476 --> 14:56.486 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

14:57.826 --> 14:59.656 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

14:59.656 --> 15:01.336 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek, New York Times.

15:01.466 --> 15:04.266 align:middle
You kind of alluded to
this earlier, but I wonder

15:04.266 --> 15:07.016 align:middle
in the event that you
see several months

15:07.016 --> 15:10.226 align:middle
of very weak headline
inflation numbers

15:10.306 --> 15:12.476 align:middle
because oil prices are
coming down so much,

15:12.516 --> 15:15.466 align:middle
but core inflation continues
to be strong or even picks up,

15:15.466 --> 15:17.466 align:middle
I wonder how you would
think about that.

15:18.526 --> 15:20.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:20.026 --> 15:22.596 align:middle
So it's hard to deal
with hypotheticals,

15:22.596 --> 15:24.136 align:middle
but I just would say this.

15:24.136 --> 15:26.626 align:middle
We, you know, we
would look at both,

15:27.126 --> 15:31.056 align:middle
and we would ask-we'd be asking
ourselves, are we confident

15:31.726 --> 15:34.026 align:middle
that inflation is on a
path down to 2 percent?

15:34.666 --> 15:35.586 align:middle
That's really the question.

15:35.786 --> 15:39.566 align:middle
And we'll be making-you know,
our policy stance will be set

15:39.566 --> 15:43.446 align:middle
at a level, ultimately,
at which we are confident

15:43.866 --> 15:46.526 align:middle
that inflation is going to
be moving down to 2 percent.

15:47.396 --> 15:50.066 align:middle
So you would-it would
depend on a lot of things.

15:50.066 --> 15:53.756 align:middle
Of course, as I mentioned, core
inflation is a better predictor

15:53.756 --> 15:55.016 align:middle
of inflation going forward.

15:55.346 --> 15:57.606 align:middle
Headline inflation
tends to be volatile.

15:57.606 --> 15:59.206 align:middle
So, in ordinary times, you,

15:59.206 --> 16:02.256 align:middle
you look through volatile
moves in commodities.

16:03.066 --> 16:05.806 align:middle
The problem with the
current situation is that,

16:05.956 --> 16:08.846 align:middle
that if you have a sustained
period of supply shocks,

16:09.696 --> 16:16.326 align:middle
those can actually start to
undermine or to work-to work

16:16.326 --> 16:18.966 align:middle
on de-anchoring inflation
expectations.

16:18.966 --> 16:21.546 align:middle
The public doesn't
distinguish between core

16:21.546 --> 16:23.036 align:middle
and headline inflation
in their thinking.

16:23.036 --> 16:25.176 align:middle
So it's something we have
to take into consideration

16:25.746 --> 16:29.226 align:middle
in our policymaking even though
our tools don't really work on,

16:30.066 --> 16:33.126 align:middle
on some aspects of this, which
are the supply-side issues.

16:34.446 --> 16:35.336 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

16:35.486 --> 16:36.556 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

16:36.556 --> 16:37.266 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

16:37.266 --> 16:39.286 align:middle
Thanks for taking my
question, Mr. Chairman.

16:39.286 --> 16:41.466 align:middle
Earlier this week, the President
said, "We are not going

16:41.466 --> 16:42.486 align:middle
to be in a recession."

16:42.936 --> 16:44.446 align:middle
So I have two questions
off of that.

16:44.446 --> 16:46.346 align:middle
Do you share the
President's confidence

16:46.346 --> 16:47.526 align:middle
in not being in a recession?

16:48.026 --> 16:52.056 align:middle
And, second, how would or would
not a recession change policy?

16:52.056 --> 16:54.466 align:middle
Is it a bright line, sir,
where, where contraction

16:54.466 --> 16:56.986 align:middle
of the economy would be a
turning point in policy?

16:56.986 --> 16:58.906 align:middle
Or is there some
amount of contraction

16:58.906 --> 17:02.096 align:middle
of the economy the Committee
would be willing to abide

17:02.436 --> 17:04.736 align:middle
in its effort to
reduce inflation?

17:05.606 --> 17:06.186 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:06.346 --> 17:11.946 align:middle
So, as I-as I mentioned,
we think it's necessary

17:11.946 --> 17:14.646 align:middle
to have growth slow down.

17:14.646 --> 17:17.396 align:middle
And growth is going to
be slowing down this year

17:17.546 --> 17:21.046 align:middle
for a couple of reasons, one of
which is that you're coming off

17:21.046 --> 17:26.036 align:middle
of the very high growth of
the reopening year of 2021.

17:27.656 --> 17:30.386 align:middle
You're also seeing
tighter monetary policy.

17:30.516 --> 17:31.896 align:middle
And you should see some slowing.

17:31.896 --> 17:35.886 align:middle
We actually think we need a
period of growth below potential

17:36.096 --> 17:38.006 align:middle
in order to create
some slack so that-so

17:38.006 --> 17:39.326 align:middle
that the supply side
can catch up.

17:39.996 --> 17:42.526 align:middle
We also think that there
will be, in all likelihood,

17:42.526 --> 17:44.286 align:middle
some softening in labor
market conditions.

17:44.946 --> 17:47.326 align:middle
And those are things
that we expect.

17:47.596 --> 17:50.846 align:middle
And we think that they're
probably necessary if we were

17:50.846 --> 17:53.676 align:middle
to have-to get inflation-if
we were to be able

17:53.676 --> 17:55.716 align:middle
to get inflation back down
on a path to 2 percent

17:55.716 --> 17:56.666 align:middle
and ultimately get there.

17:59.086 --> 17:59.576 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

17:59.926 --> 18:03.376 align:middle
The question was whether you
see a recession coming and how

18:03.376 --> 18:04.636 align:middle
that might or might
not change policy.

18:04.946 --> 18:05.696 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

18:05.696 --> 18:07.866 align:middle
So we're going to be-again,
we're going to be focused

18:07.866 --> 18:09.156 align:middle
on getting inflation back down.

18:09.276 --> 18:13.756 align:middle
And we-as I've said
on other occasions,

18:14.336 --> 18:18.546 align:middle
price stability is really
the bedrock of the economy.

18:18.836 --> 18:21.466 align:middle
And nothing works in the
economy without price stability.

18:21.496 --> 18:23.866 align:middle
We can't have a strong labor
market without price stability

18:23.866 --> 18:25.096 align:middle
for an extended period of time.

18:25.526 --> 18:26.946 align:middle
We all want to get
back to the kind

18:26.946 --> 18:29.886 align:middle
of labor market we had
before the pandemic,

18:30.236 --> 18:32.986 align:middle
where differences
between, you know,

18:32.986 --> 18:36.016 align:middle
racial and gender differences
and that kind of thing were

18:36.016 --> 18:39.406 align:middle
at historic minimums, where
participation was high,

18:39.406 --> 18:40.676 align:middle
where inflation was low.

18:40.676 --> 18:43.306 align:middle
We want to get back to that,
but that's not happening.

18:43.306 --> 18:44.796 align:middle
That's not going
to happen without,

18:45.016 --> 18:46.436 align:middle
without restoring
price stability.

18:46.566 --> 18:49.876 align:middle
So that's something we
see as, as something

18:49.876 --> 18:51.566 align:middle
that we simply must do.

18:51.926 --> 18:55.566 align:middle
And we think that in the-we
don't see it as a tradeoff with,

18:56.186 --> 18:58.106 align:middle
with the employment mandate.

18:58.106 --> 19:00.686 align:middle
We see it as a way to facilitate
the sustained achievement

19:00.686 --> 19:02.636 align:middle
of the employment mandate
in the longer term.

19:03.856 --> 19:05.146 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:05.756 --> 19:06.496 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

19:06.496 --> 19:07.446 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

19:07.816 --> 19:10.296 align:middle
Particularly in regard
to expectations,

19:10.296 --> 19:12.696 align:middle
it's been said the last
few months that the risks

19:12.696 --> 19:16.006 align:middle
of doing too little
outweighed the lists-the risks

19:16.006 --> 19:16.806 align:middle
of doing too much.

19:16.806 --> 19:18.276 align:middle
Does that remain the bias?

19:18.826 --> 19:20.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:20.306 --> 19:22.686 align:middle
So we're trying to do just
the right amount, right?

19:22.826 --> 19:24.766 align:middle
We're not-we're not
trying to have a recession.

19:24.766 --> 19:25.856 align:middle
And we don't think we have to.

19:26.346 --> 19:29.786 align:middle
We think that there's a path for
us to be able to bring inflation

19:29.786 --> 19:32.896 align:middle
down while sustaining a strong
labor market, as I mentioned,

19:33.326 --> 19:36.346 align:middle
along with-in all
likelihood-some, some softening

19:36.346 --> 19:37.446 align:middle
in labor market conditions.

19:37.636 --> 19:40.116 align:middle
So that is-that's what
we're trying to achieve,

19:40.116 --> 19:42.176 align:middle
and we continue to think
that there's a path to that.

19:42.226 --> 19:45.706 align:middle
We know that the path has, has
clearly narrowed, really based

19:45.706 --> 19:47.556 align:middle
on events that are
outside of our control.

19:47.556 --> 19:48.476 align:middle
And it may narrow further.

19:49.156 --> 19:57.416 align:middle
So, you know, I do think-as I
said just now-that restoring

19:57.416 --> 19:59.946 align:middle
price stability is just
something that we have to do.

19:59.946 --> 20:02.276 align:middle
There, there isn't an option
to fail to do that because

20:02.276 --> 20:03.866 align:middle
that is the thing
that enables you

20:03.866 --> 20:05.586 align:middle
to have a strong labor
market over time.

20:05.586 --> 20:09.416 align:middle
Without restoring price
stability, you won't be able,

20:09.786 --> 20:11.146 align:middle
over the medium and longer term,

20:11.186 --> 20:15.096 align:middle
to actually have a
strong-a sustained period

20:15.096 --> 20:16.506 align:middle
of very strong labor
market conditions.

20:16.506 --> 20:19.156 align:middle
So, of course, we serve both
sides of the dual mandate,

20:19.156 --> 20:21.126 align:middle
but we actually see them
as well aligned on this.

20:21.596 --> 20:21.946 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

20:21.946 --> 20:24.536 align:middle
But as a-as a follow-on to
that, given the uncertainty

20:24.536 --> 20:26.406 align:middle
and that sort of
paradoxical flow

20:26.406 --> 20:29.406 align:middle
of data you've been getting, if
you're going to make a mistake,

20:29.906 --> 20:32.776 align:middle
would you rather make the
mistake on doing too much,

20:32.846 --> 20:35.296 align:middle
raising too much, than
raising too little?

20:36.226 --> 20:36.996 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:36.996 --> 20:38.336 align:middle
We're trying not
to make a mistake.

20:38.336 --> 20:39.356 align:middle
Let me put it this way:

20:39.356 --> 20:42.186 align:middle
We do see that there
are two-sided risks.

20:42.186 --> 20:46.026 align:middle
There would be the risk of
doing too much and, you know,

20:46.106 --> 20:48.616 align:middle
imposing more of a downturn
on the economy than,

20:48.686 --> 20:51.146 align:middle
than was necessary,
but the risk-the risk

20:51.146 --> 20:53.796 align:middle
of doing too little
and leaving the economy

20:53.796 --> 20:56.606 align:middle
with this entrenched inflation,
it only raises the cost.

20:56.606 --> 20:58.796 align:middle
If you fail to deal with
it in the near term,

20:58.796 --> 21:02.086 align:middle
it only raises the cost of,
of dealing with it later

21:02.696 --> 21:05.446 align:middle
to the extent people start
to see it as just part

21:05.446 --> 21:06.486 align:middle
of their economic lives.

21:06.486 --> 21:08.246 align:middle
They start to factor
high inflation

21:08.246 --> 21:11.426 align:middle
into their decisions-when
that-on a sustained basis.

21:11.976 --> 21:13.436 align:middle
When that starts
to really happen,

21:13.616 --> 21:14.806 align:middle
and we don't think
that's happened yet,

21:14.806 --> 21:17.896 align:middle
but when that starts to happen,
it just gets that much harder.

21:18.416 --> 21:19.856 align:middle
And the pain will be
that much greater.

21:20.036 --> 21:22.096 align:middle
So I really do think
that it's important

21:22.096 --> 21:25.276 align:middle
that we-that we address
this now and get it done.

21:26.276 --> 21:26.796 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:27.546 --> 21:29.096 align:middle
Neil. NEIL IRWIN.

21:29.096 --> 21:29.936 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

21:29.936 --> 21:31.176 align:middle
Neil Irwin from Axios.

21:31.176 --> 21:33.656 align:middle
To build a little bit on
what Steve was asking,

21:34.916 --> 21:37.016 align:middle
do you believe the United States
is currently in a recession?

21:37.626 --> 21:39.626 align:middle
Will the GDP reading
tomorrow affect

21:39.626 --> 21:40.856 align:middle
that judgment one
way or the other?

21:41.226 --> 21:42.436 align:middle
And has your assessment
of the risk

21:42.436 --> 21:44.386 align:middle
of recession changed
any in recent weeks?

21:44.846 --> 21:45.706 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:45.856 --> 21:49.566 align:middle
So I don't-I do not think
the U.S. is currently

21:49.566 --> 21:50.216 align:middle
in a recession.

21:51.036 --> 21:54.266 align:middle
And the reason is, there
are just too many areas

21:54.266 --> 21:59.606 align:middle
of the economy that are-that are
performing, you know, too well.

21:59.606 --> 22:02.716 align:middle
And, of course, I would point to
the labor market, in particular.

22:02.856 --> 22:06.136 align:middle
As I mentioned, it's true
that growth is slowing

22:06.596 --> 22:08.646 align:middle
for reasons that we understand.

22:08.646 --> 22:11.406 align:middle
Really, the growth was
extraordinarily high last year,

22:11.406 --> 22:12.346 align:middle
5½ percent.

22:12.746 --> 22:14.096 align:middle
We would have expected
growth to slow.

22:14.096 --> 22:15.876 align:middle
There's also more
slowing going on now.

22:16.276 --> 22:17.466 align:middle
But if you look at
the labor market,

22:17.466 --> 22:18.686 align:middle
you've got growth-I think,

22:18.796 --> 22:21.846 align:middle
payroll jobs averaging
450,000 per month.

22:22.336 --> 22:24.586 align:middle
That's a remarkably
strong level for,

22:24.586 --> 22:26.616 align:middle
for this state of, of affairs.

22:27.076 --> 22:30.096 align:middle
The unemployment rate at near
a 50-year low at 3.6 percent.

22:30.516 --> 22:34.486 align:middle
All of the wage measures that we
track are running very strong.

22:34.486 --> 22:36.096 align:middle
So this is a very
strong labor market,

22:36.626 --> 22:39.066 align:middle
and it's just not
consistent with-you know,

22:39.066 --> 22:42.216 align:middle
2.7 million people hired in
the first half of the year.

22:42.266 --> 22:45.246 align:middle
It doesn't make sense
that the economy would be

22:45.246 --> 22:47.226 align:middle
in recession with, with this
kind of thing happening.

22:47.556 --> 22:50.636 align:middle
So I don't think
the U.S. economy's

22:50.636 --> 22:51.566 align:middle
in recession right now.

22:51.926 --> 22:52.956 align:middle
NEIL IRWIN.

22:54.036 --> 22:54.596 align:middle
GDP? GDP tomorrow?

22:54.596 --> 22:55.066 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:55.276 --> 22:57.166 align:middle
Ah, haven't seen it, and,

22:57.166 --> 22:58.506 align:middle
and we'll just have
to see what it says.

22:58.616 --> 23:01.206 align:middle
I don't-I mean, I
would say, generally,

23:01.446 --> 23:04.386 align:middle
the GDP numbers do
have a tendency

23:04.386 --> 23:05.996 align:middle
to be revised pretty
significantly.

23:05.996 --> 23:09.036 align:middle
It's just-it's just that
it's very hard-it's very hard

23:09.036 --> 23:10.226 align:middle
to cumulate [to an
estimate of] U.S. GDP.

23:10.226 --> 23:11.336 align:middle
It's a large economy.

23:11.336 --> 23:13.976 align:middle
And a lot of-a lot of work
and judgment goes into that.

23:14.016 --> 23:17.006 align:middle
But you tend to take first
GDP reports, I think,

23:17.056 --> 23:18.836 align:middle
with a-with a grain of salt.

23:18.896 --> 23:20.466 align:middle
But, of course, it's
something we'll be looking at.

23:20.756 --> 23:21.376 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:22.026 --> 23:25.336 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

23:25.906 --> 23:26.406 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

23:26.406 --> 23:27.656 align:middle
Victoria Guida with Politico.

23:27.656 --> 23:30.506 align:middle
I wanted to ask about the new
conflict-of-interest rules

23:30.506 --> 23:31.476 align:middle
that you all rolled out.

23:31.476 --> 23:34.296 align:middle
Some senators have written
asking for those rules

23:34.296 --> 23:37.426 align:middle
to have more teeth and to
have sort of more transparency

23:37.426 --> 23:40.976 align:middle
about Fed's financial-the Fed
officials' financial activity.

23:41.056 --> 23:42.626 align:middle
I was wondering if you
could speak to that

23:42.626 --> 23:43.876 align:middle
and whether you intend
on, you know,

23:44.226 --> 23:45.986 align:middle
toughening those
rules in any way.

23:46.526 --> 23:46.766 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:46.766 --> 23:49.056 align:middle
So those are-those are the
toughest rules in place,

23:49.526 --> 23:52.056 align:middle
you know, at any comparable
institution that I'm aware of.

23:52.646 --> 23:55.456 align:middle
We think-you know, we've
thought about this carefully,

23:55.456 --> 23:56.876 align:middle
and we put them in place.

23:56.876 --> 24:00.366 align:middle
And we're building the
infrastructure so that-so that,

24:01.296 --> 24:03.576 align:middle
you know, the enforcement
will be tight, that, actually,

24:03.576 --> 24:06.226 align:middle
you won't be able to make trades
unless they're precleared.

24:06.976 --> 24:11.706 align:middle
And the amount of trades you
make, they'll have to be 45 days

24:11.706 --> 24:14.146 align:middle
or more before any FOMC meeting.

24:14.146 --> 24:18.596 align:middle
It's just-I think we've really,
you know, created a very strong

24:18.596 --> 24:21.496 align:middle
and robust set of rules
that will, you know,

24:21.496 --> 24:23.516 align:middle
support public trust
in the institution.

24:23.736 --> 24:27.546 align:middle
And, again, we're just
now-the system is just now

24:27.546 --> 24:28.116 align:middle
up and running.

24:28.116 --> 24:29.386 align:middle
And I'm proud of what we did.

24:29.846 --> 24:31.926 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:32.106 --> 24:33.936 align:middle
Catarina. CATARINA SARAIVA.

24:33.936 --> 24:35.216 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

24:35.386 --> 24:37.876 align:middle
Hi. Catarina Saraiva,
Bloomberg News.

24:37.946 --> 24:40.506 align:middle
I wanted to ask a
little bit more

24:40.506 --> 24:45.106 align:middle
about the 75 basis points
versus 50 versus 25.

24:45.166 --> 24:49.056 align:middle
Can you talk a little bit
about what, what kind of goes

24:49.346 --> 24:53.026 align:middle
into your thinking for, you
know, making the decision

24:53.026 --> 24:57.296 align:middle
on how much to raise rates
and, you know, just talking

24:57.296 --> 25:00.436 align:middle
about a very large amount
that you alluded to?

25:00.776 --> 25:04.176 align:middle
Could that possibly
be 100 basis points?

25:04.176 --> 25:07.806 align:middle
And then, kind of along those
lines, is there any sort

25:08.646 --> 25:12.976 align:middle
of weakness in the economy,
outside of inflation measures,

25:12.976 --> 25:17.076 align:middle
that would lead you to
slow your hiking pace?

25:18.216 --> 25:18.426 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:18.926 --> 25:22.356 align:middle
So I'm going to-I'm going
to take that as a question

25:22.356 --> 25:24.216 align:middle
about the next meeting
and thereafter.

25:24.216 --> 25:27.986 align:middle
So, as I mentioned, we're
going to be looking at all

25:27.986 --> 25:30.506 align:middle
of those things: activity,
labor market, inflation.

25:30.506 --> 25:33.006 align:middle
And we're going to be thinking
about our policy stance

25:33.006 --> 25:34.486 align:middle
and where does it
really need to be.

25:35.086 --> 25:37.696 align:middle
And I also mentioned that, as
this process-now that we're

25:37.696 --> 25:40.126 align:middle
at neutral-as the process
goes on, at some point,

25:40.746 --> 25:42.896 align:middle
it will be appropriate
to, to slow down.

25:43.176 --> 25:45.766 align:middle
And we haven't made a
decision when that point is,

25:45.766 --> 25:47.496 align:middle
but intuitively that
makes sense, right?

25:47.496 --> 25:50.416 align:middle
We've been front-end-loading
these very large rate increases,

25:50.416 --> 25:52.236 align:middle
and now we're getting closer
to where we need to be.

25:52.966 --> 25:55.086 align:middle
So that's how we're
thinking about it.

25:55.086 --> 25:56.986 align:middle
And in terms of September,
we're going-we're going

25:56.986 --> 26:00.386 align:middle
to watch the data and the
evolving outlook very carefully

26:00.786 --> 26:02.946 align:middle
and factor in everything
and make a decision

26:02.946 --> 26:04.146 align:middle
in September about what to do.

26:04.286 --> 26:06.986 align:middle
And I'm not really going to
provide any specific guidance

26:06.986 --> 26:07.936 align:middle
about what that might be.

26:08.736 --> 26:11.346 align:middle
So, you know, but I mentioned

26:11.346 --> 26:14.296 align:middle
that we might do another
unusually large rate increase.

26:14.296 --> 26:16.276 align:middle
But that's not a decision
that we've made at all.

26:16.326 --> 26:19.506 align:middle
So we are-you know, we're
going to be guided by the data.

26:19.826 --> 26:23.486 align:middle
And I think you can still
think of the, the destination

26:23.486 --> 26:26.436 align:middle
as broadly in line with the
September-sorry, the June SEP,

26:27.096 --> 26:28.916 align:middle
because it's only six weeks old.

26:28.916 --> 26:31.916 align:middle
And sometimes, sometimes SEPs
can get old really quick.

26:32.436 --> 26:36.296 align:middle
I think in this one-this
one I would say is probably,

26:36.296 --> 26:38.376 align:middle
probably the best
guide we have as to

26:38.376 --> 26:41.376 align:middle
where the Committee thinks
it needs to get at the end

26:41.376 --> 26:42.846 align:middle
of the year and then
into next year.

26:42.846 --> 26:43.986 align:middle
So I would point you to that.

26:45.186 --> 26:46.046 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

26:47.096 --> 26:48.026 align:middle
Chris. CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

26:48.026 --> 26:48.646 align:middle
Hi. Thank you.

26:48.986 --> 26:50.606 align:middle
Chris Rugaber, Associated Press.

26:50.606 --> 26:56.426 align:middle
I wanted to ask about right now,
there's a sort of growing gap

26:56.426 --> 26:59.486 align:middle
between the Fed's preferred
measure of inflation, the PCE,

26:59.826 --> 27:03.116 align:middle
PCE index, and the
one that's followed

27:03.116 --> 27:05.216 align:middle
by the public, the
CPI, of course.

27:05.726 --> 27:09.826 align:middle
How do you expect to handle this
divergence if the PCE starts

27:09.906 --> 27:14.386 align:middle
to come down enough for you to
consider slowing rate hikes,

27:14.566 --> 27:17.846 align:middle
even if the public is still
seeing much higher CPI ratings?

27:18.476 --> 27:18.746 align:middle
Thank you.

27:18.916 --> 27:19.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:19.576 --> 27:21.566 align:middle
So it's an interesting,
interesting situation.

27:21.566 --> 27:24.636 align:middle
Of course, we've,
we've long used PCE

27:24.636 --> 27:25.896 align:middle
because we think
it's just better

27:25.896 --> 27:27.546 align:middle
at capturing the inflation

27:27.806 --> 27:29.476 align:middle
that people actually
face in their lives.

27:30.166 --> 27:32.686 align:middle
And I think that that view
is pretty widely understood.

27:32.686 --> 27:34.906 align:middle
That said, the public
really reads about CPI.

27:34.906 --> 27:36.406 align:middle
And the difference really is

27:36.406 --> 27:40.126 align:middle
because the CPI has higher
weights on things like food,

27:40.126 --> 27:44.246 align:middle
gasoline, motor vehicles, and
housing than the PCE index does.

27:44.546 --> 27:46.246 align:middle
And so that accounts for
a lot of the difference.

27:46.516 --> 27:49.956 align:middle
However, over time, they
tend to come together.

27:49.956 --> 27:53.316 align:middle
You know, we-given the
importance in the public eye

27:53.316 --> 27:55.786 align:middle
of CPI, we are calling
it out and noticing it

27:55.786 --> 27:56.466 align:middle
and everything like that.

27:56.466 --> 27:58.886 align:middle
But remember, we do target PCE.

27:59.416 --> 28:00.976 align:middle
That is because we think
it's a better measure.

28:01.536 --> 28:03.636 align:middle
They will come together
eventually.

28:03.636 --> 28:04.896 align:middle
The typical gap was really

28:04.896 --> 28:07.326 align:middle
about 25 basis points
for a very long time.

28:07.436 --> 28:10.516 align:middle
And if it went-if it
got to 40 basis points,

28:10.516 --> 28:11.516 align:middle
that would be very noticeable.

28:11.516 --> 28:13.706 align:middle
And now it's-now it's
much larger than that

28:13.706 --> 28:15.136 align:middle
because of the things
I mentioned.

28:15.726 --> 28:18.126 align:middle
So we'll be watching
both, but, again,

28:18.126 --> 28:21.256 align:middle
the one that we think is the
best measure always has been

28:21.256 --> 28:25.766 align:middle
PCE, at least since I think
we-some 20 plus years ago-moved

28:25.766 --> 28:26.256 align:middle
to PCE.

28:26.646 --> 28:27.666 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

28:27.756 --> 28:28.736 align:middle
Michael McKee.

28:30.366 --> 28:31.416 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

28:31.866 --> 28:34.766 align:middle
I'd like to weave a couple of
things together, Mr. Chairman.

28:34.766 --> 28:37.306 align:middle
Michael McKee from
Bloomberg TV and Radio.

28:38.056 --> 28:40.966 align:middle
You said that the destination
pretty much remains the same

28:40.966 --> 28:43.376 align:middle
in terms of your
end-of-the-year target.

28:43.866 --> 28:45.766 align:middle
But where are you
in the journey?

28:47.036 --> 28:50.116 align:middle
We've now seen the federal
stimulus programs end.

28:50.116 --> 28:53.256 align:middle
You mentioned consumer spending,
business investment have slowed.

28:53.676 --> 28:57.416 align:middle
Are they moving at a
pace you would expect?

28:57.536 --> 29:01.766 align:middle
Or is demand still greater
than supply-too much greater

29:01.766 --> 29:04.626 align:middle
than supply that you need
to do significantly more?

29:04.826 --> 29:08.496 align:middle
And I ask because there
are lags in the impact

29:08.496 --> 29:10.026 align:middle
of monetary policy,
as you mentioned.

29:10.436 --> 29:13.246 align:middle
And a lot of this
might hit in 2023.

29:13.356 --> 29:16.016 align:middle
The strong dollar
effect may hit in 2023,

29:16.586 --> 29:18.636 align:middle
when the economy might be weak.

29:18.766 --> 29:20.696 align:middle
How, how do you know
where you are

29:20.736 --> 29:23.416 align:middle
and where you think
you need to get to?

29:24.026 --> 29:24.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:24.936 --> 29:28.056 align:middle
Well, just talking about demand
for a second: As I mentioned

29:28.056 --> 29:32.916 align:middle
in my remarks, I think you
pretty clearly do see a slowing

29:32.916 --> 29:36.876 align:middle
now in demand in the second
quarter-consumer spending,

29:36.996 --> 29:39.476 align:middle
business fixed investment,
housing, places like that.

29:40.216 --> 29:43.026 align:middle
I think, you know,
people widely looked

29:43.096 --> 29:46.226 align:middle
at the first-quarter numbers and
thought they didn't make sense

29:46.286 --> 29:47.946 align:middle
and might have been
misleading in terms

29:47.946 --> 29:50.706 align:middle
of the overall direction of
the economy-[that was] not,

29:50.776 --> 29:52.106 align:middle
not true of the second quarter.

29:52.426 --> 29:55.326 align:middle
But at the same time, you
have this labor market.

29:55.326 --> 29:56.986 align:middle
So there are plenty
of experiences

29:56.986 --> 30:00.916 align:middle
where GDP has been
reported as weak

30:00.916 --> 30:02.596 align:middle
and the labor market as strong.

30:02.896 --> 30:05.526 align:middle
And the economy has gone right
through that and been fine.

30:05.826 --> 30:07.986 align:middle
So that-that's happened
many times.

30:07.986 --> 30:10.636 align:middle
And it used to happen-if you
remember, in the first quarter

30:10.636 --> 30:13.676 align:middle
of every year, for several years
in a row, GDP was negative,

30:14.256 --> 30:16.146 align:middle
and the labor market
was moving on just fine.

30:16.146 --> 30:17.806 align:middle
And it turned out to just
be measurement error.

30:17.906 --> 30:19.506 align:middle
It was called residual
seasonality.

30:19.996 --> 30:21.576 align:middle
We don't know the situation.

30:21.576 --> 30:24.776 align:middle
The truth is, though, we, we
think that demand is moderating.

30:24.916 --> 30:26.936 align:middle
We do. How much is
it moderating?

30:27.086 --> 30:27.656 align:middle
We're not sure.

30:27.916 --> 30:29.476 align:middle
We're going to have to
watch the data carefully.

30:29.636 --> 30:32.546 align:middle
There are-there is a
great deal of money

30:32.546 --> 30:34.396 align:middle
on people's balance
sheets that they can spend.

30:34.766 --> 30:36.026 align:middle
The unemployment
rate is very low.

30:36.166 --> 30:37.336 align:middle
The labor market's very hot.

30:37.336 --> 30:39.006 align:middle
There are many, many
job openings.

30:39.206 --> 30:39.936 align:middle
Wages are high.

30:39.936 --> 30:42.976 align:middle
So it's the kind of
thing where you think

30:42.976 --> 30:45.166 align:middle
that the-that the economy
should actually be,

30:45.856 --> 30:47.366 align:middle
be doing pretty well in the
second half of the year.

30:47.906 --> 30:48.736 align:middle
But we'll have to see.

30:48.736 --> 30:51.646 align:middle
We don't know that because
you do see a marked slowing

30:51.646 --> 30:54.516 align:middle
in the second quarter that
does-that is fairly broad.

30:54.516 --> 30:55.686 align:middle
So we'll be watching that.

30:56.046 --> 30:56.756 align:middle
We'll be watching that.

30:56.756 --> 30:58.196 align:middle
Of course, as I mentioned,

30:58.196 --> 31:01.416 align:middle
we do want to see demand
running below potential

31:01.946 --> 31:04.116 align:middle
for a sustained period
to create slack and,

31:04.166 --> 31:06.256 align:middle
and give inflation a
chance to come down.

31:06.766 --> 31:07.636 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:07.856 --> 31:12.466 align:middle
Nicole. NICOLE GOODKIND.

31:12.546 --> 31:13.436 align:middle
Hi, thank you, Chairman.

31:13.436 --> 31:14.956 align:middle
Nicole Goodkind, CNN Business.

31:15.426 --> 31:17.556 align:middle
When does the Committee expect

31:17.666 --> 31:20.086 align:middle
to see a meaningful
slowdown in the labor market?

31:20.086 --> 31:22.906 align:middle
And how much weakness
will it accept with regard

31:22.906 --> 31:26.976 align:middle
to slower job growth and higher
unemployment before it pauses

31:26.976 --> 31:28.916 align:middle
or begins to think
about cutting rates?

31:29.656 --> 31:30.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:31.846 --> 31:34.866 align:middle
So I think you're already
seeing-you've seen,

31:34.866 --> 31:38.006 align:middle
in the labor market, what
you've seen is a decline

31:38.006 --> 31:40.586 align:middle
from very high levels of
job creation last year

31:40.586 --> 31:41.566 align:middle
and earlier this year

31:42.046 --> 31:45.486 align:middle
to modestly slower
job creation-still,

31:45.636 --> 31:47.046 align:middle
still quite robust,
as I mentioned.

31:47.446 --> 31:48.306 align:middle
So you're seeing that.

31:49.076 --> 31:52.756 align:middle
You're seeing some increase-some
increase in initial claims

31:52.756 --> 31:55.806 align:middle
from insurance, although
that may actually have to do

31:55.806 --> 31:57.856 align:middle
with seasonal adjustments.

31:57.856 --> 31:59.536 align:middle
We're not sure that
that's actually real.

32:00.556 --> 32:02.916 align:middle
There's some evidence
that wages-if you look

32:02.916 --> 32:05.416 align:middle
at average hourly earnings,
they appear to be moderating,

32:06.166 --> 32:08.366 align:middle
not so yet from the
other wage measures.

32:08.366 --> 32:10.526 align:middle
And we'll be getting the
employment compensation index

32:10.686 --> 32:12.706 align:middle
measurement, I think,
on Friday, I guess.

32:13.126 --> 32:15.206 align:middle
And that's a very important
one because it adjusts

32:15.206 --> 32:17.116 align:middle
for compensation-composition.

32:17.116 --> 32:21.176 align:middle
So I'd say you-and
also, anecdotally,

32:21.176 --> 32:27.406 align:middle
you hear much-the sort of
level of concern on the part

32:27.406 --> 32:29.906 align:middle
of businesses that they simply
can't find workers is probably

32:29.906 --> 32:34.166 align:middle
down a little bit from what
it was, say, for example,

32:34.166 --> 32:35.556 align:middle
late in the latter
parts of last year.

32:35.556 --> 32:40.356 align:middle
So there's a feeling that the
labor market is moving back

32:40.356 --> 32:41.036 align:middle
into balance.

32:41.356 --> 32:44.716 align:middle
If you look at-if you look
at job openings or quits,

32:44.716 --> 32:47.096 align:middle
you see them moving sideways
or perhaps a little bit down.

32:47.436 --> 32:49.496 align:middle
But it's only the
beginning of an adjustment.

32:49.646 --> 32:53.136 align:middle
But I think most-also,
if you look at-I mean,

32:53.296 --> 32:55.116 align:middle
once you start citing these
things, you can't stop.

32:55.636 --> 32:58.166 align:middle
If you look at the
household survey,

32:58.166 --> 32:59.986 align:middle
you see much lower job creation,

32:59.986 --> 33:02.536 align:middle
and the household survey
can be quite volatile,

33:02.536 --> 33:05.246 align:middle
but it has no jobs created
in the last three months.

33:05.246 --> 33:06.436 align:middle
So that might be a signal

33:07.156 --> 33:09.226 align:middle
that job creation's
actually a little bit slower

33:09.226 --> 33:12.646 align:middle
than we're seeing in the-in
the establishment survey.

33:12.746 --> 33:17.166 align:middle
So executive summary [is]: I
would say there's some evidence

33:17.356 --> 33:22.226 align:middle
that labor demand may be slowing
a bit-labor supply, not so much.

33:22.226 --> 33:23.306 align:middle
We have been disappointed

33:23.306 --> 33:25.746 align:middle
that labor force participation
really hasn't moved

33:25.746 --> 33:27.416 align:middle
up since January.

33:27.826 --> 33:31.406 align:middle
That may be related to yet
another big wave of COVID.

33:31.716 --> 33:33.446 align:middle
And there's evidence
that that's the case.

33:33.486 --> 33:35.366 align:middle
But-So we're not seeing much
in the way of labor supply.

33:35.786 --> 33:39.846 align:middle
Nonetheless, I would say
some, some progress on demand

33:39.846 --> 33:41.356 align:middle
and supply getting
back in alignment.

33:41.356 --> 33:41.776 align:middle
NICOLE GOODKIND.

33:41.776 --> 33:43.836 align:middle
And then how much weakness
would you be willing

33:44.436 --> 33:46.356 align:middle
to accept before you think
about pausing rate cuts?

33:46.356 --> 33:46.566 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:46.686 --> 33:48.236 align:middle
So I think we're
looking-we're going

33:48.236 --> 33:52.186 align:middle
to be looking at, at
inflation as well.

33:52.186 --> 33:53.466 align:middle
You know, as I mentioned,

33:53.466 --> 33:55.426 align:middle
we need to see inflation
coming down.

33:55.916 --> 33:58.796 align:middle
We need to be confident that
inflation is going to get back

33:58.796 --> 34:00.426 align:middle
down to mandate-consistent
levels.

34:01.126 --> 34:03.766 align:middle
That's not something
we can avoid doing.

34:03.896 --> 34:05.916 align:middle
That really needs to happen.

34:06.326 --> 34:10.186 align:middle
And, but we do think, though,
that the labor market can adjust

34:10.876 --> 34:14.666 align:middle
because of the huge
overhang of job openings,

34:15.276 --> 34:16.536 align:middle
of excess demand, really.

34:16.766 --> 34:19.916 align:middle
There should be able
to be an adjustment

34:20.886 --> 34:24.326 align:middle
that would have lower
than-perhaps lower-than-expected

34:25.006 --> 34:27.406 align:middle
increases in unemployment-lower
than would be expected

34:27.406 --> 34:32.996 align:middle
in the ordinary course of events
because the level-the ratio of,

34:33.206 --> 34:36.026 align:middle
of vacancies to unemployed
is just out of keeping

34:36.026 --> 34:37.276 align:middle
with historical experience.

34:37.276 --> 34:39.566 align:middle
And that suggests that this
time could be different.

34:39.566 --> 34:40.166 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

34:40.966 --> 34:41.686 align:middle
Edward Lawrence.

34:43.236 --> 34:43.656 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

34:43.656 --> 34:45.086 align:middle
Thanks, Mr. Chairman.

34:45.366 --> 34:47.046 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business.

34:47.046 --> 34:49.226 align:middle
So you said that the
path may be narrowed

34:49.466 --> 34:50.756 align:middle
to avoiding a recession.

34:50.756 --> 34:52.916 align:middle
So how, how close are
we to a recession?

34:52.916 --> 34:55.636 align:middle
And then how do forecasts of
possible recession from banks

34:55.636 --> 34:56.876 align:middle
and economists, how does

34:56.876 --> 34:59.456 align:middle
that make a soft landing you've
talked about more difficult?

34:59.956 --> 35:00.766 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:00.766 --> 35:04.876 align:middle
So as, as I mentioned,
it doesn't seem

35:04.876 --> 35:06.966 align:middle
that the U.S. economy is
in recession right now.

35:07.086 --> 35:09.106 align:middle
And I think you do
see weakening,

35:09.706 --> 35:14.576 align:middle
some slowdown-let's put
it that way-in growth.

35:14.956 --> 35:17.876 align:middle
And you see it across some of
the categories that I mentioned.

35:17.876 --> 35:20.806 align:middle
But there's also just the
very strong data coming

35:20.806 --> 35:21.946 align:middle
out of the labor market, still.

35:22.606 --> 35:27.126 align:middle
So, overall, you would say
that, in all probability,

35:27.126 --> 35:32.196 align:middle
demand is still strong, and, and
the economy is still on track

35:32.196 --> 35:33.306 align:middle
to continue to grow this year.

35:34.036 --> 35:38.476 align:middle
But the slowdown in the
second quarter is notable.

35:38.476 --> 35:40.786 align:middle
And we're-you know, we're going
to be watching that carefully.

35:40.786 --> 35:41.996 align:middle
Sorry, what was the-
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

35:41.996 --> 35:42.546 align:middle
Forecasts.

35:42.546 --> 35:45.196 align:middle
You know, how do bank forecasts
of possible recessions,

35:45.196 --> 35:50.196 align:middle
economists, does that
affect the soft landing?

35:50.806 --> 35:52.096 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:52.796 --> 35:55.156 align:middle
Well, you know, we've said since
the beginning, I think, that,

35:55.356 --> 35:57.816 align:middle
that having a soft landing
is what we're aiming for.

35:57.816 --> 35:59.086 align:middle
Of course, that has
to be our goal.

35:59.086 --> 35:59.656 align:middle
It is our goal.

36:00.466 --> 36:02.256 align:middle
And we'll keep trying
to achieve it.

36:02.576 --> 36:06.646 align:middle
I think-I do think
events-at the beginning,

36:06.646 --> 36:08.586 align:middle
we said it was not
going to be easy.

36:08.586 --> 36:10.266 align:middle
It was going to be quite
challenging to do that.

36:10.816 --> 36:11.586 align:middle
It's unusual.

36:12.016 --> 36:12.876 align:middle
It's an unusual event.

36:12.876 --> 36:16.166 align:middle
It's not a typical
event, given where we are.

36:16.486 --> 36:18.336 align:middle
If there is a path to it,
and we think there is,

36:18.336 --> 36:21.826 align:middle
it is the one I mentioned, that
the labor market actually is-has

36:21.826 --> 36:24.226 align:middle
such a large amount
of surplus demand

36:24.846 --> 36:30.006 align:middle
that you could see-you could see
that demand coming down in a way

36:30.006 --> 36:33.686 align:middle
that didn't translate into a
big increase in unemployment

36:33.686 --> 36:35.766 align:middle
as you would expect in the
ordinary course because,

36:35.846 --> 36:38.886 align:middle
frankly, the imbalance
is so much greater.

36:39.146 --> 36:40.776 align:middle
And, but we don't know that.

36:40.776 --> 36:43.176 align:middle
I mean, this is a-this is
a case of first impression.

36:43.596 --> 36:46.476 align:middle
So anyone who is really
sure that it's impossible

36:46.576 --> 36:50.016 align:middle
or really sure that it
will happen is probably

36:50.236 --> 36:51.836 align:middle
underestimating the
level of uncertainty.

36:52.046 --> 36:54.026 align:middle
And so I would certainly
say it's an uncertain,

36:54.746 --> 36:55.446 align:middle
uncertain thing.

36:55.676 --> 36:57.706 align:middle
Nonetheless, it's our
goal to achieve it,

36:57.756 --> 37:01.646 align:middle
and we'll keep trying
to do that.

37:03.696 --> 37:04.396 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

37:04.396 --> 37:04.916 align:middle
Jean. JEAN YUNG.

37:04.916 --> 37:06.036 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

37:06.036 --> 37:07.616 align:middle
Jean Yung with Market News.

37:08.116 --> 37:10.756 align:middle
I wanted to ask about the
balance sheet reduction program.

37:10.956 --> 37:12.956 align:middle
It's been working for
a couple months now

37:13.316 --> 37:14.626 align:middle
and in a different environment

37:14.626 --> 37:16.376 align:middle
than the last time
the Fed did it.

37:17.056 --> 37:18.976 align:middle
What are you learning
about how it's working

37:18.976 --> 37:20.356 align:middle
and how markets are reacting?

37:21.026 --> 37:24.146 align:middle
And is reaching the minimum
level of reserves needed

37:24.146 --> 37:26.736 align:middle
in the system still several
years away at the current pace?

37:26.736 --> 37:27.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:27.286 --> 37:28.876 align:middle
So we think it's working fine.

37:28.876 --> 37:32.186 align:middle
As you know, we, we
tapered up into it.

37:32.316 --> 37:35.216 align:middle
And, and in September,
we'll go to full strength.

37:35.916 --> 37:37.496 align:middle
And the markets seem
to have accepted it.

37:37.716 --> 37:43.476 align:middle
By all assessments, the markets
should be able to absorb this.

37:43.476 --> 37:44.886 align:middle
And we expect that'll
be the case.

37:45.606 --> 37:49.286 align:middle
So I would say the plan
is broadly on track.

37:49.286 --> 37:51.826 align:middle
It's a little bit slow
to get going because some

37:51.826 --> 37:55.286 align:middle
of these trades don't settle
until-for a bit of time.

37:55.286 --> 37:56.566 align:middle
But it'll be picking up steam.

37:57.366 --> 38:03.376 align:middle
So I guess your second
question was getting-the process

38:03.376 --> 38:07.736 align:middle
of getting back down to the new
equilibrium will take a while.

38:08.296 --> 38:12.436 align:middle
And that time, it's hard to
be precise, but, you know,

38:12.436 --> 38:15.446 align:middle
the model would suggest that
it could be between two,

38:15.446 --> 38:17.986 align:middle
two and a half years,
that kind of thing.

38:17.986 --> 38:20.496 align:middle
And this is a much faster
pace than the last time.

38:20.596 --> 38:23.326 align:middle
Balance sheet's much,
much bigger than it was.

38:23.426 --> 38:26.456 align:middle
But we looked at this carefully,
and we thought that this,

38:27.026 --> 38:28.256 align:middle
this was the sensible pace.

38:28.256 --> 38:29.776 align:middle
And we have no reason
to think it's not.

38:31.086 --> 38:31.756 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:33.526 --> 38:34.426 align:middle
Brian. BRIAN CHEUNG.

38:34.836 --> 38:35.956 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Powell.

38:36.056 --> 38:37.266 align:middle
Brian Cheung, Yahoo Finance.

38:37.266 --> 38:39.656 align:middle
Looking at financial conditions,

38:39.656 --> 38:42.386 align:middle
it seems like the tightening
has slowed as of late

38:42.436 --> 38:44.006 align:middle
with the 10 year coming down,

38:44.076 --> 38:46.616 align:middle
30-year fixed mortgage rates
also going a bit sideways,

38:46.616 --> 38:48.276 align:middle
when we talk about a hot
housing market still.

38:48.626 --> 38:50.986 align:middle
Just wondering if you find
financial conditions tight

38:50.986 --> 38:53.076 align:middle
enough, especially as you
continue to raise rates.

38:53.796 --> 38:54.316 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:54.376 --> 38:57.396 align:middle
So a big piece of that is
inflation expectations-you know,

38:57.396 --> 39:00.596 align:middle
breakevens coming down, which,
you know, is a good thing.

39:00.596 --> 39:03.636 align:middle
It's a good thing that markets
do seem to have confidence in,

39:03.706 --> 39:05.216 align:middle
in the Committee's commitment

39:05.696 --> 39:08.366 align:middle
to getting inflation
back down to 2 percent.

39:08.366 --> 39:10.756 align:middle
So we like to see
market-based, you know,

39:10.756 --> 39:13.146 align:middle
readings of inflation
expectations come down.

39:13.776 --> 39:15.976 align:middle
You know, broader financial
conditions have tightened,

39:16.196 --> 39:17.516 align:middle
tightened a good bit.

39:17.636 --> 39:20.846 align:middle
I mean, the way this works
is we, we set our policy,

39:21.286 --> 39:23.036 align:middle
and financial conditions react,

39:23.036 --> 39:25.116 align:middle
and then financial conditions
are what affects the economy.

39:25.116 --> 39:26.956 align:middle
And we don't control
that second step.

39:27.726 --> 39:30.276 align:middle
We're just going to do what
we think needs to be done.

39:30.466 --> 39:33.116 align:middle
We're going to-we're going to
get our policy rate to a level

39:33.146 --> 39:34.186 align:middle
where we're confident

39:34.256 --> 39:36.666 align:middle
that inflation will be moving
back down to 2 percent.

39:36.756 --> 39:37.216 align:middle
Confident.

39:37.766 --> 39:39.316 align:middle
So that's how we're
going to take it.

39:39.316 --> 39:41.606 align:middle
And, of course, we'll be
watching financial conditions

39:41.606 --> 39:44.386 align:middle
to see that they are
appropriately tight

39:44.586 --> 39:45.916 align:middle
and that they're
having the effect

39:46.496 --> 39:47.606 align:middle
that we would hope
they're having,

39:47.976 --> 39:49.916 align:middle
which is to see demand moderate

39:49.976 --> 39:51.896 align:middle
and inflationary
pressures recede and,

39:51.896 --> 39:53.016 align:middle
ultimately, inflation come down.

39:54.726 --> 39:56.206 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:57.586 --> 39:59.616 align:middle
Greg. GREG ROBB.

40:00.966 --> 40:01.826 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

40:02.016 --> 40:03.246 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

40:03.746 --> 40:06.176 align:middle
I wonder if you can go
back a little bit in time

40:06.176 --> 40:09.396 align:middle
to before there was this
outbreak of inflation,

40:09.396 --> 40:13.266 align:middle
when the Committee put
in place forward guidance

40:13.316 --> 40:15.186 align:middle
on tapering asset purchases.

40:15.186 --> 40:16.806 align:middle
I think it was December 2020.

40:17.836 --> 40:20.316 align:middle
There's a recent speech by
Fed Governor Waller talking

40:20.316 --> 40:23.346 align:middle
about that and saying that
maybe that was too tight,

40:23.666 --> 40:26.066 align:middle
that kind of condition,

40:26.066 --> 40:29.806 align:middle
and it put you a little bit
behind-not his words but maybe,

40:29.806 --> 40:34.146 align:middle
you know, were kind of late to
react to some of that inflation.

40:34.146 --> 40:36.886 align:middle
So could you talk about that
decision, and have you looked

40:36.886 --> 40:37.996 align:middle
at it in hindsight at all?

40:38.526 --> 40:39.536 align:middle
Thank you.

40:39.576 --> 40:39.926 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:40.026 --> 40:43.326 align:middle
So, yes, we said that
we wouldn't lift off

40:43.326 --> 40:46.286 align:middle
until we had basically
achieved our dual-mandate goals.

40:46.866 --> 40:51.006 align:middle
And the reason we did it in real
time was that, the first look

40:51.006 --> 40:53.946 align:middle
at the new framework
that we'd rolled out,

40:54.826 --> 40:57.266 align:middle
plenty of people were
saying, "It's not credible.

40:57.266 --> 40:58.776 align:middle
You'll never get
inflation to 2 percent."

40:58.776 --> 41:01.486 align:middle
Some of our critics now who say
inflation's too tight-too high

41:01.486 --> 41:02.516 align:middle
were the same ones
who were saying,

41:02.516 --> 41:03.626 align:middle
"You'll never get to 2 percent."

41:03.666 --> 41:06.536 align:middle
Well, but anyway,
that's what happened.

41:06.536 --> 41:09.076 align:middle
So we, we thought we needed

41:09.076 --> 41:12.176 align:middle
to really make a strong
statement with that.

41:12.176 --> 41:13.266 align:middle
It wasn't part of the framework.

41:13.466 --> 41:16.236 align:middle
The December '20
guidance was not part

41:16.236 --> 41:17.576 align:middle
of our overall new framework.

41:17.576 --> 41:19.226 align:middle
It was just guidance
that we put in place.

41:19.816 --> 41:22.796 align:middle
So I would say two things.

41:22.856 --> 41:26.266 align:middle
One, I don't think that
that has materially changed

41:26.266 --> 41:27.026 align:middle
the situation.

41:27.126 --> 41:29.846 align:middle
But I have to admit, I don't
think I would do that again.

41:29.966 --> 41:31.526 align:middle
I don't think I would
do that again.

41:31.526 --> 41:33.986 align:middle
We-you know, ultimately,
the situation evolved

41:33.986 --> 41:36.306 align:middle
in a highly unexpected
way for all of us.

41:37.006 --> 41:41.276 align:middle
And, you know, maybe
the learning is

41:41.276 --> 41:43.886 align:middle
that leave a little more
flexibility than that.

41:44.506 --> 41:46.376 align:middle
But did it matter in the end?

41:46.376 --> 41:49.156 align:middle
You know, we-if you look at-I
really don't think it did.

41:49.156 --> 41:50.346 align:middle
I'm not sure it would've
mattered

41:50.346 --> 41:52.566 align:middle
if we'd been raising rates
three months earlier.

41:52.566 --> 41:54.356 align:middle
Does anybody think that
would've made a big difference?

41:54.356 --> 41:57.186 align:middle
I mean, lots of central banks
were raising rates three months

41:57.186 --> 41:59.076 align:middle
earlier, and it didn't matter.

41:59.146 --> 42:02.406 align:middle
I mean, this is a-this is
a global phenomenon that's

42:02.406 --> 42:03.656 align:middle
happening now-admittedly,

42:03.656 --> 42:05.976 align:middle
different in the United
States, but anyway.

42:06.106 --> 42:06.496 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:06.496 --> 42:08.336 align:middle
Nancy. GREG ROBB.

42:09.256 --> 42:10.066 align:middle
Can I follow up?

42:10.066 --> 42:10.686 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:10.766 --> 42:11.486 align:middle
Sorry about that, Greg.

42:12.126 --> 42:13.466 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

42:13.616 --> 42:16.286 align:middle
Just a lot of people talk
about that time, and they talk

42:16.286 --> 42:17.446 align:middle
about a taper tantrum.

42:17.446 --> 42:19.556 align:middle
I know you haven't
talked about it too much

42:19.556 --> 42:22.536 align:middle
but that you were worried
at the time about repeating

42:22.536 --> 42:25.546 align:middle
that taper tantrum that you
had experienced as a Governor.

42:25.846 --> 42:27.176 align:middle
So was that part of that?

42:27.886 --> 42:29.396 align:middle
Where did that fit into
all those decisions?

42:29.696 --> 42:29.936 align:middle
Thank you.

42:30.416 --> 42:30.626 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:30.626 --> 42:34.026 align:middle
So I think we learned-there've
been multiple taper tantrums,

42:34.026 --> 42:34.266 align:middle
right?

42:34.266 --> 42:37.066 align:middle
So there was the
famous one in 2013.

42:37.656 --> 42:40.806 align:middle
There's what happened at the
December '18 meeting, where,

42:41.436 --> 42:44.216 align:middle
you know, markets can
ignore developments

42:44.216 --> 42:45.806 align:middle
around the balance
sheet for years on end

42:46.126 --> 42:49.206 align:middle
and then suddenly
react very sharply.

42:49.286 --> 42:53.286 align:middle
So we just had developed a
practice of moving predictably

42:53.286 --> 42:54.856 align:middle
and doing it in steps
and things like that.

42:54.856 --> 42:56.366 align:middle
It was just like
that's how we did it.

42:56.806 --> 42:58.126 align:middle
And so we did it
that way this time.

42:58.126 --> 43:01.896 align:middle
We were-we were careful to,
to take steps and communicate

43:01.896 --> 43:02.926 align:middle
and all that kind of thing.

43:02.926 --> 43:04.956 align:middle
Yes, we were trying
to avoid a tantrum

43:04.956 --> 43:06.416 align:middle
because they can be
quite destructive.

43:06.416 --> 43:08.126 align:middle
They can tighten
financial conditions

43:08.126 --> 43:10.376 align:middle
and knock the economy
off kilter.

43:10.376 --> 43:14.656 align:middle
And, you know, when it happens,
you have to-really, in both '13

43:14.656 --> 43:17.016 align:middle
and '18, it really
had consequences

43:17.016 --> 43:20.156 align:middle
for the real economy, you know,
two, three, four months later.

43:20.156 --> 43:21.576 align:middle
So we were trying to avoid that.

43:21.886 --> 43:22.456 align:middle
That was part of it.

43:22.926 --> 43:26.556 align:middle
Again, I don't think
that's-the real issue of 2020

43:27.116 --> 43:30.016 align:middle
and '21 was just trying to
understand what was happening

43:30.016 --> 43:31.166 align:middle
with the reopening economy.

43:31.166 --> 43:33.436 align:middle
That, that was where
the big uncertainty was.

43:33.436 --> 43:35.976 align:middle
And, you know, our view was

43:35.976 --> 43:38.566 align:middle
that these supply-side
issues would get better:

43:38.566 --> 43:40.276 align:middle
that people would
go back to work,

43:40.276 --> 43:42.216 align:middle
that labor force
participation would come back,

43:42.816 --> 43:45.456 align:middle
everyone would get
vaccinated, schools would open,

43:46.016 --> 43:47.616 align:middle
kids would be in school,

43:47.696 --> 43:50.466 align:middle
and labor force participation
would jump back up.

43:50.696 --> 43:53.846 align:middle
That's what we were-that
was very broadly thought

43:53.846 --> 43:54.456 align:middle
to be the case:

43:55.016 --> 43:58.186 align:middle
these supply-side issues would
get solved reasonably quickly.

43:58.186 --> 43:59.026 align:middle
And they just haven't.

43:59.146 --> 43:59.806 align:middle
They still haven't.

44:00.276 --> 44:02.116 align:middle
So that's really the
learning, I think,

44:02.116 --> 44:07.176 align:middle
is around how complicated these
supply-side issues can be.

44:07.176 --> 44:09.726 align:middle
We haven't seen this
before in a long, long time.

44:09.776 --> 44:13.666 align:middle
And so that's really, you know,
what accounts for the pace

44:13.666 --> 44:15.176 align:middle
at which we moved, and we did.

44:15.626 --> 44:19.366 align:middle
When inflation changed
direction, really, in October,

44:19.566 --> 44:21.116 align:middle
we've moved quickly since then.

44:21.116 --> 44:22.216 align:middle
I think people would agree.

44:22.636 --> 44:24.936 align:middle
But before then, inflation was
coming down month by month.

44:24.936 --> 44:26.006 align:middle
And, you know, we kind

44:26.086 --> 44:28.576 align:middle
of thought we had the
story-probably had the

44:28.576 --> 44:29.166 align:middle
story right.

44:29.226 --> 44:33.526 align:middle
But then, I think in October,
you started to see a range

44:33.526 --> 44:34.876 align:middle
of data that said "No.

44:34.876 --> 44:38.266 align:middle
This is a much stronger economy
and much higher inflation

44:38.636 --> 44:39.416 align:middle
than we've been thinking."

44:39.416 --> 44:41.686 align:middle
And, again, we've
pivoted, and here we are.

44:41.686 --> 44:41.896 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

44:41.896 --> 44:43.636 align:middle
Thanks. MICHELLE SMITH.

44:43.806 --> 44:45.346 align:middle
Nancy. NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

44:45.346 --> 44:46.726 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

44:46.726 --> 44:49.486 align:middle
Chair Powell, I just want to
pin you down a little bit more

44:49.486 --> 44:51.036 align:middle
on the issue of recession.

44:51.536 --> 44:54.326 align:middle
So if we get a negative
GDP number tomorrow

44:54.376 --> 44:55.306 align:middle
for the second quarter,

44:55.306 --> 44:58.036 align:middle
would the Fed consider
the U.S. in a recession?

44:58.376 --> 45:01.646 align:middle
And just remind us,
what is your definition

45:01.906 --> 45:03.086 align:middle
for the start of a recession?

45:03.216 --> 45:03.486 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:03.486 --> 45:05.666 align:middle
So the Fed doesn't make
a-doesn't make a judgment

45:05.666 --> 45:05.966 align:middle
on that.

45:06.026 --> 45:08.896 align:middle
You know, we're focused on the
dual mandate and using our tools

45:08.896 --> 45:10.746 align:middle
to achieve maximum
employment and price stability.

45:10.746 --> 45:13.416 align:middle
We don't-we don't say
"There is now a recession"

45:13.416 --> 45:14.046 align:middle
and that kind of thing.

45:14.046 --> 45:15.706 align:middle
So that wouldn't be
something we'd do.

45:15.706 --> 45:19.166 align:middle
We would look at the data
tomorrow-and no doubt,

45:19.406 --> 45:21.766 align:middle
we'll look at it very
carefully-and draw whatever

45:21.766 --> 45:22.886 align:middle
implications we can.

45:22.886 --> 45:27.196 align:middle
As I mentioned, though,
you know, if you think

45:27.196 --> 45:31.216 align:middle
about what an inflation-sorry,
what a recession really is,

45:31.216 --> 45:34.776 align:middle
it's a broad-based decline
across many industries that,

45:34.856 --> 45:38.006 align:middle
you know, is sustained for
more than a couple of months,

45:38.006 --> 45:40.016 align:middle
and there are a bunch
of specific tests in it.

45:40.016 --> 45:42.976 align:middle
And it just doesn't-this just
doesn't seem like that now.

45:42.976 --> 45:44.836 align:middle
What we have right now
doesn't seem like that.

45:44.836 --> 45:47.856 align:middle
And the real reason is that the
labor market is just sending

45:47.856 --> 45:50.256 align:middle
such a signal of
economic strength

45:50.796 --> 45:53.516 align:middle
that it makes you really
question the GDP data.

45:53.766 --> 45:57.176 align:middle
But, again, that's not a
decision that we-that we make.

45:57.176 --> 45:58.796 align:middle
And we won't-we won't
reach a conclusion one way

45:58.796 --> 46:00.106 align:middle
or another on that.

46:00.696 --> 46:01.046 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:02.376 --> 46:04.256 align:middle
Simon. SIMON RABINOVITCH.

46:04.256 --> 46:05.016 align:middle
Thank you very much.

46:05.016 --> 46:06.856 align:middle
Simon Rabinovitch
with the Economist.

46:07.236 --> 46:09.966 align:middle
You said that some softening
in the labor market is needed.

46:10.236 --> 46:13.176 align:middle
Within the Fed, there are
staff economists who've argued

46:13.176 --> 46:15.946 align:middle
that NAIRU, the noninflationary
rate of unemployment,

46:16.346 --> 46:18.486 align:middle
might be as high as 5
to 6 percent right now,

46:18.486 --> 46:22.436 align:middle
which obviously is a lot higher
than the current rate of 3.6.

46:22.536 --> 46:24.356 align:middle
What's your assessment of NAIRU?

46:24.356 --> 46:25.756 align:middle
Is that something that came

46:25.756 --> 46:26.906 align:middle
up in discussion
with the Committee?

46:26.906 --> 46:27.606 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:28.126 --> 46:33.536 align:middle
So I think, broadly, a
lot of economists think

46:33.596 --> 46:37.166 align:middle
that the-that the natural rate
of unemployment will have moved

46:37.856 --> 46:42.736 align:middle
up to some significant extent

46:42.736 --> 46:44.916 align:middle
above where we think
it was before.

46:44.946 --> 46:48.476 align:middle
And the reason is there's-it's
the usual matching issue,

46:48.476 --> 46:51.426 align:middle
where matching has become
less effective, and that kind

46:51.426 --> 46:54.016 align:middle
of thing when you have the
kind of turbulent downturn

46:54.016 --> 46:56.566 align:middle
and the big switches
in demand from services

46:56.566 --> 46:58.336 align:middle
to goods and all that.

46:58.406 --> 47:00.166 align:middle
So it could be higher.

47:00.276 --> 47:03.276 align:middle
And my own instinct is
that the natural rate

47:03.276 --> 47:04.436 align:middle
of unemployment is higher.

47:04.986 --> 47:07.176 align:middle
Of course, I would add
that we don't know it.

47:07.176 --> 47:09.856 align:middle
We can never know where
these star variables are

47:10.046 --> 47:12.396 align:middle
in real time with
any confidence.

47:12.446 --> 47:14.606 align:middle
But I would say it must
have moved up materially.

47:14.836 --> 47:19.756 align:middle
But the other side of that is,
as all these jobs get created

47:19.756 --> 47:23.116 align:middle
and people go back to work
and unemployment is so low,

47:23.336 --> 47:26.556 align:middle
I think you could, in principle,
you could see it coming back

47:26.606 --> 47:28.046 align:middle
down pretty significantly.

47:28.576 --> 47:31.316 align:middle
And that would also, by the way,
take pressure off of inflation

47:31.876 --> 47:35.766 align:middle
because it's that gap-it's the
gap between actual unemployment

47:35.766 --> 47:40.356 align:middle
and the-and the natural rate
that really is relevant for,

47:40.956 --> 47:43.286 align:middle
you know, the negative
slack we have in the economy

47:43.286 --> 47:45.046 align:middle
with the overtight labor market.

47:45.046 --> 47:47.436 align:middle
So if you were to-you
wouldn't observe this.

47:47.436 --> 47:50.056 align:middle
It's an unobservable, but
you could actually be seeing

47:50.056 --> 47:52.216 align:middle
inflationary pressures coming
down if that does happen.

47:52.216 --> 47:54.036 align:middle
And we don't-we don't
control that.

47:54.076 --> 48:00.056 align:middle
But that's something that,
logically, if the pandemic

48:00.056 --> 48:03.516 align:middle
and the disorder in the labor
market caused the natural rate

48:03.516 --> 48:06.216 align:middle
to move up, then as the
labor market settles down,

48:06.216 --> 48:08.366 align:middle
in principle, you should
see it move back down.

48:09.306 --> 48:09.746 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

48:10.636 --> 48:14.726 align:middle
Jeff. JEFF COX.

48:16.296 --> 48:17.146 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

48:17.146 --> 48:19.086 align:middle
Jeff Cox from CNBC.com.

48:19.086 --> 48:21.296 align:middle
Just a question about-hate

48:21.396 --> 48:23.366 align:middle
to keep banging the
drum about recession.

48:23.366 --> 48:26.256 align:middle
But most of the polls that we're
seeing now from the public,

48:26.976 --> 48:29.416 align:middle
people believe that we're
already in a recession

48:29.416 --> 48:32.546 align:middle
or heading for one-economists,
pretty much the same thing.

48:33.266 --> 48:36.686 align:middle
They're being told by folks
like you and the Administration

48:36.686 --> 48:38.666 align:middle
that we're not in a
recession, we're not heading

48:38.666 --> 48:41.246 align:middle
for a recession-frankly,
coming from the same people

48:41.246 --> 48:43.816 align:middle
who told them inflation was
transitory, telling them now

48:43.816 --> 48:45.116 align:middle
that we're not heading
for a recession.

48:45.376 --> 48:48.236 align:middle
So what would you tell the
public to reassure them now

48:48.236 --> 48:51.486 align:middle
that you feel confident in
your forecast going forward

48:51.486 --> 48:52.986 align:middle
and the Fed is ready to respond

48:52.986 --> 48:56.166 align:middle
to a-to a potential
downturn in the economy?

48:56.676 --> 48:57.166 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:57.166 --> 49:00.316 align:middle
No, all I've really said is
I don't think it's likely

49:00.316 --> 49:02.466 align:middle
that the U.S. economy's
in a recession now.

49:02.676 --> 49:04.406 align:middle
And I've explained
why that is the case.

49:04.536 --> 49:06.976 align:middle
It's, it's because you do see
a very strong labor market.

49:06.976 --> 49:08.736 align:middle
And I think the public
will see that as well.

49:09.546 --> 49:14.646 align:middle
You know, going forward, what
we've seen is a-is a slowing

49:14.646 --> 49:16.596 align:middle
in spending, as I mentioned.

49:17.396 --> 49:22.186 align:middle
We've seen the very beginnings
of perhaps a slight lessening

49:22.186 --> 49:25.136 align:middle
in the-in the tightness
of the labor market,

49:25.136 --> 49:26.546 align:middle
but it would only
be the beginnings.

49:27.146 --> 49:33.866 align:middle
So, and I mentioned that, that-I
also said that our goal is

49:33.866 --> 49:37.756 align:middle
to bring inflation down and
have a so-called soft landing,

49:37.756 --> 49:40.086 align:middle
by which I mean a landing

49:40.086 --> 49:42.216 align:middle
that doesn't require a
significant increase,

49:42.216 --> 49:44.006 align:middle
a really significant
increase in unemployment.

49:44.866 --> 49:47.136 align:middle
We're trying to achieve that.

49:47.306 --> 49:50.966 align:middle
I have said, on many occasions,
that we understand that's going

49:50.966 --> 49:53.116 align:middle
to be quite challenging and
that it's got more challenging

49:53.696 --> 49:54.616 align:middle
over recent months.

49:55.976 --> 49:56.716 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

49:56.716 --> 49:58.526 align:middle
Kyle. KYLE CAMPBELL.

49:59.456 --> 50:00.266 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

50:00.396 --> 50:02.266 align:middle
Kyle Campbell with
American Banker.

50:03.216 --> 50:06.536 align:middle
The FOMC has historically
tried to avoid the kind

50:06.536 --> 50:08.926 align:middle
of rapid monetary
policy tightening

50:09.586 --> 50:11.556 align:middle
that has happened
so far this year.

50:12.346 --> 50:14.476 align:middle
How concerned are you
that the rate hikes

50:14.556 --> 50:17.246 align:middle
that we've seen thus
far might increase risks

50:17.246 --> 50:20.976 align:middle
to financial stability not just
domestically, but globally?

50:22.416 --> 50:23.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:23.336 --> 50:24.676 align:middle
I mean, there are precedents

50:24.676 --> 50:28.146 align:middle
for the FOMC moving very
quickly-for example, in 1994,

50:28.146 --> 50:29.556 align:middle
and in 1980, even more so.

50:29.966 --> 50:31.356 align:middle
So we've been known to do

50:31.356 --> 50:32.816 align:middle
that when it's the
appropriate thing to do.

50:32.816 --> 50:34.996 align:middle
And this year, it clearly is.

50:34.996 --> 50:37.946 align:middle
I would say that, given
how quickly we've moved,

50:38.166 --> 50:43.036 align:middle
I'm gratified that
while-that, basically,

50:43.036 --> 50:44.556 align:middle
markets have been working.

50:44.556 --> 50:45.316 align:middle
They've been orderly.

50:45.986 --> 50:48.476 align:middle
There's been some volatility,
but that's only to be expected.

50:49.006 --> 50:52.586 align:middle
For a financial stability
perspective, you know,

50:52.586 --> 50:56.636 align:middle
asset values are down,
which in some-in some sense

50:56.636 --> 50:58.186 align:middle
lowers vulnerabilities.

50:58.846 --> 51:01.066 align:middle
It's when they're really
high that you would worry

51:01.066 --> 51:03.296 align:middle
that they're-that they're
vulnerable to a fall, actually.

51:03.556 --> 51:06.536 align:middle
Many asset values
have come down.

51:06.536 --> 51:08.696 align:middle
I think you've got a
well-capitalized banking system.

51:08.846 --> 51:12.976 align:middle
I think you have-households are
generally in about as strong

51:12.976 --> 51:16.796 align:middle
as financial shape as they've
been in a very long time

51:16.796 --> 51:19.806 align:middle
or perhaps ever given
the money that's

51:19.806 --> 51:21.056 align:middle
on people's balance sheets.

51:22.306 --> 51:25.506 align:middle
So you have a pretty-from a
financial stability standpoint,

51:25.506 --> 51:28.616 align:middle
you have a pretty
decent picture.

51:28.986 --> 51:31.916 align:middle
Now, the macroeconomic,
there are plenty

51:31.916 --> 51:34.306 align:middle
of macroeconomic issues
that don't rise to the level

51:34.306 --> 51:35.766 align:middle
of financial stability concerns.

51:35.796 --> 51:38.276 align:middle
By financial stability, you
know, we think of that as things

51:38.276 --> 51:40.456 align:middle
that might undermine the
working of the financial system,

51:40.536 --> 51:41.926 align:middle
so big, serious things.

51:42.456 --> 51:45.146 align:middle
That's not to say that
people at the lower end

51:45.146 --> 51:46.466 align:middle
of the income spectrum aren't,

51:46.546 --> 51:47.956 align:middle
aren't suffering,
because they are.

51:47.956 --> 51:49.266 align:middle
They're suffering
from high inflation.

51:49.676 --> 51:55.166 align:middle
They're going to the-to the
grocery store and finding that,

51:55.466 --> 51:59.346 align:middle
in many cases, their paycheck
doesn't cover the food they're

51:59.346 --> 52:00.026 align:middle
accustomed to buying.

52:00.026 --> 52:01.476 align:middle
We're seeing actual, you know,

52:01.536 --> 52:03.496 align:middle
real declines in
food consumption.

52:03.496 --> 52:05.116 align:middle
And, you know, it's
very concerning.

52:05.786 --> 52:07.606 align:middle
It's very unfortunate.

52:07.606 --> 52:10.116 align:middle
And that's why we're-we're
really committed to bringing

52:10.116 --> 52:12.456 align:middle
down inflation-one
of the reasons.

52:12.826 --> 52:13.456 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

52:13.456 --> 52:17.016 align:middle
Let's go to Mark for
the last question.

52:17.256 --> 52:17.666 align:middle
MARK HAMRICK.

52:17.666 --> 52:18.936 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

52:18.936 --> 52:20.326 align:middle
Mark Hamrick with Bankrate.

52:20.876 --> 52:23.396 align:middle
I can remember when you held
your first news conference,

52:23.396 --> 52:27.746 align:middle
and you vowed to be a
very plainspoken Chairman,

52:27.856 --> 52:32.246 align:middle
and we're thinking today about
the impacts of Fed policy

52:32.246 --> 52:33.616 align:middle
on individuals as well.

52:33.616 --> 52:36.256 align:middle
What would you say to
individuals or households

52:36.256 --> 52:39.226 align:middle
who may yet lose their jobs
in this tightening cycle

52:39.226 --> 52:42.496 align:middle
in the fight against
inflation as they try

52:42.496 --> 52:45.256 align:middle
to translate what Fed
policy means to them

52:45.256 --> 52:47.186 align:middle
in this complicated
economic landscape?

52:47.256 --> 52:47.676 align:middle
Thank you.

52:48.096 --> 52:48.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

52:48.306 --> 52:50.746 align:middle
So I guess the first
thing I would say to,

52:50.966 --> 52:54.966 align:middle
to every household is that we
know that inflation is too high.

52:54.966 --> 52:58.466 align:middle
We understand how painful it
is, particularly for people

52:58.466 --> 53:01.246 align:middle
who are living paycheck
to paycheck and spend most

53:01.246 --> 53:05.296 align:middle
of that paycheck on necessities,
such as food and gas

53:05.816 --> 53:08.716 align:middle
and heating their homes and
clothing and things like that.

53:08.716 --> 53:11.876 align:middle
We do understand that, that
those people suffer the most.

53:12.346 --> 53:16.606 align:middle
Middle-class and better-off
people have some resources,

53:16.606 --> 53:17.846 align:middle
where they can absorb
these things.

53:17.846 --> 53:20.086 align:middle
But people, many people,
don't have those resources.

53:20.086 --> 53:22.856 align:middle
So, you know, we-it is our job.

53:22.856 --> 53:24.476 align:middle
It is our institutional role.

53:24.676 --> 53:26.436 align:middle
We are assigned uniquely

53:26.546 --> 53:29.676 align:middle
and unconditionally
the obligation of,

53:30.236 --> 53:33.216 align:middle
of providing price stability
to the American people.

53:33.626 --> 53:35.316 align:middle
And we're going to use
our tools to do that.

53:36.066 --> 53:39.176 align:middle
As I mentioned, there will
be some, in all likelihood,

53:39.176 --> 53:41.156 align:middle
some softening in labor
market conditions.

53:41.616 --> 53:45.886 align:middle
We need growth to slow to
below potential growth.

53:45.886 --> 53:50.406 align:middle
We don't want to-we
don't want this

53:50.406 --> 53:53.146 align:middle
to be bigger than
it needs to be.

53:53.146 --> 53:56.086 align:middle
But, ultimately, if you
think about the medium

53:56.086 --> 53:59.296 align:middle
and longer term, price
stability is the thing that,

53:59.366 --> 54:01.046 align:middle
that makes the whole
economy work.

54:01.046 --> 54:04.226 align:middle
It's what can-it can give us a
strong labor market and wages

54:04.226 --> 54:06.536 align:middle
that aren't being eaten
up by high inflation.

54:06.716 --> 54:09.716 align:middle
If you talk to people-again,
people who are making, you know,

54:09.716 --> 54:13.686 align:middle
wages, relatively low
wages-they're the ones

54:13.686 --> 54:15.196 align:middle
who are suffering the
most from inflation.

54:15.196 --> 54:19.146 align:middle
So it's all the more reason why
we need to move, move on this.

54:19.886 --> 54:20.466 align:middle
Thank you very much.

